\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Sing dollar rises above 1.80, first since May 24. \b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2001\par 09:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 26 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar strengthened to 1.80 against the U.S. dollar for the first time since May 24 on Thursday, aided by a simmering of the Indonesian political crisis. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said U.S. funds and local banks were dumping their U.S. dollar positions amassed during the height of Indonesia's political crisis and the economic turmoil in Argentina. \par \par \uc2  "The market has found the situation in Indonesia is stabilising so there is an expectation the rupiah will strengthen to 9,000," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesia rupiah hit 11-week highs around 9,800 on Wednesday, as the market reacted positively to Tuesday's sacking of Wahid Abdurrahman as president. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said funds might also be reassessing their favourable view toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months in light of poor corporate earnings from the U.S and little sign the U.S. economy will recover soon. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar has also recently slipped against the yen and euro as the market wondered whether a change may be afoot in the long-standing strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  But U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill has repeated his commitment to the policy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said heavy U.S. dollar sales from funds triggered stop-loss orders at 1.8090-1.8100 and 1.8050, pushing the Singapore dollar to highs around 1.7998 from 1.8169 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The European bank dealer added interbank players also started trimming long U.S. dollar positions on fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  This in turn opened the floodgates for more U.S. dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the Singapore dollar would have trouble clearing resistance around 1.7980 as players were looking to buy dollars on dips and added it would correct back to around 1.8050. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is unquestionably a buy at these levels, but I think the market is going to take some time to settle after the violent move today. A lot of people have got burnt today and quite badly," said a local bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Charts showed the dollar is oversold with a 14-day relative strength index reading at 15.67. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  both in the spot and money markets last week to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it changed its policy stance earlier this month to neutral from tightening. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar had been under pressure given the country's deteriorating economic fundamentals, losing around 3.5 percent since January. It touched an 11-year low of 1.8398 on July 12. \par \par \uc2  The city state is now technically in recession after advance second-quarter GDP fell 10.1 percent on an annnualised quarter-on-quarter basis. The government has slashed its economic growth forecast for 2001 to just 0.5-1.5 percent from 3.5-5.5 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010726dx7q00fl5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
