\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Mixed on Indonesia, steady yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 928 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2001\par 04:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 23 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a mixed bag early Monday as the southeast Asia markets warily eyed developments in Indonesia while the northen markets followed the yen's lead. \par \par \uc2  Caution prevailed as the Indonesian parliament meets to impeach President Abdurrahman Wahid in defiance of his declaration of a state of emergency early Monday. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah jumped to 10,400 after the Supreme Court ruled that Wahid's declaration was illegal. \par \par \uc2  A peaceful impeachment of Wahid could lead to a further rally in the rupiah, but analysts said that may have been already priced in while the risk of violence and weak economic fundamentals will cap significant gains. Volumes in either case will be tiny, they said. \par \par \uc2  Early losses in the Singapore dollar, the most liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Southeast Asia, reflected those worries. \par \par \uc2  The yen was slightly off its New York highs. But lingering doubts over the strong dollar policy's fate despite President George W. Bush's assurances during the weekend's Group of Eight summit was expected to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under pressure. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah bounced to 10,400 from 11,125 as the market betted that President Wahid would soon be kicked out of office. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta dealers said local and foreign banks unloaded dollars as moves against Wahid gathered steam. But volumes were extremely thin, which exaggerated the move higher. \par \par \uc2  The bid/ask spread was wide, indicating players are reluctant to trade. Dealers added, however, local corporate clients may emerge around these levels to snap up dollars given their debt servicing needs. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia said \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policy remained unchanged after the state of emergency. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The market jerked the Singapore dollar lower in reaction to the Indonesian political crisis. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Sing dollar slipped past the 1.8300 mark in Sydney in early trade, but was saved by players looking to take quick profit on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  They added the market was eyeing the yen's direction for trading cues. \par \par \uc2  But caution dictated the market with talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the local dollar on Friday during London and New York trade. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso slipped on local corporate dollar demand, though dealers hesitated in testing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve in defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Manila dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was viewed as keen on capping the peso's losses ahead of President Gloria Arroyo's State of the Nation address. \par \par \uc2  The bank said over the weekend it was considering stiffer penalties against speculators, who the government has blamed for the peso's recent losses. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a dearth of dollars along with poor economic fundamentals and local corporate dollar needs have driven the peso lower. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht sank as corporate players and offshore names continued to bid for dollar amid weakness in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar offers from exporters around 45.80 helped limit the baht's losses, but a decisive break below 45.85 could lead to 46.00, a level not seen since March 1998. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's foreign reserves fell to $31.5 billion as of July 13, below the minimum of $32-34 billion suggested by the IMF. \par \par \uc2  Baht funding costs in the offshore market were easier, but remained high, with the one-month forward rate at 19/27 satang per dollar and the 12-month tenor at 175/215 satang. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won tracked the yen's fall with poor sentiment in the local bourse and equity-related outflows likely to weigh on it. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said with the view on the Korean economy still bearish, players would continue to buy dollar on dips. \par \par \uc2  The Korea Development Institute last week cut its 2001 GDP forecast to 4.0 percent from 4.3 percent, saying exports will remain sluggish due to the global slowdown. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  urged financial institutions to lower their lending rates to spur investment activity. Finance Minister Jin Nyum said on Monday the government will boost spending in the second half and hoped to see an impact in the fourth quarter. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar edged up but was close to the 35 mark in cautious trade after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on both sides on Friday. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers said the market's direction would be set by June export orders and industrial output numbers due 0230 GMT and June jobless rate due 0300 GMT. \par \par \uc2  A recent Reuters poll showed economists on average expected June export orders to fall by 16.88 percent on year, and industrial output to slip by 9.21 percent on year. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0230 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     123.33       123.15         -0.15\par  Sing dlr      1.8242       1.8232         -0.05\par  Taiwan dlr    34.936       34.961         +0.07\par  Korean won   1306.00      1305.60         -0.03\par  Baht           45.80        45.75         -0.11\par  Peso           53.35        53.10         -0.47\par  Rupiah      10500.00     11138.00         +6.08\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.7995         +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     123.33       114.45         -7.20\par  Sing dlr      1.8242       1.7365         -4.81\par  Taiwan dlr    34.936       32.900         -5.83\par  Korean won   1306.00      1277.00         -2.22\par  Baht           45.80        43.70         -4.59\par  Peso           53.35        50.75         -4.87\par  Rupiah      10500.00      9470.00         -9.81\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.7995         +0.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010723dx7n000c7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
