\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late; Tracks Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 ' Gains \b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 2001\par 10:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was up late Friday, tracking gains in several Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen and the Indonesian rupiah, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0750 GMT (4.50 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8250, compared with S$1.8281 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rebounded slightly from its early morning lows of S$1.8233 as market participants took profit on stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The range for the day was S$1.8233 to S$1.8261, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's weakness against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a follow-on effect on fears the Bush Administration may back off from the strong U.S. dollar policy, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days have also limited much of the U.S. dollar's upside moves. \par \par \uc2  Market sentiment was cautious Friday as the weekend approached, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Mild profit-taking on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to continue in overnight trade with offshore markets likely to test the U.S. dollar's upper limits of S$1.8270, while support is at S$1.8220. \par \par \uc2  The stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped boost overall sentiment in regional bonds market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds were mostly higher, although trading was thin ahead of the weekend, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  The yields on two-and five-year bonds were two basis points lower at 2.16% and 2.88%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was flat at 3.31% while the yield on 10-year bonds was one basis point lower at 3.57%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web Site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps ended mostly lower, in line\par with local bond yields.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The seven-year and 10-year swap offers closed one basis point lower at\par 3.46% and 3.70%, respectively.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap was unchanged at 106\par basis points from late Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interbank money market, rates were flat to lower amid flush\par liquidity.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Friday       Late Thursday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         2.5000% 2.7500%   4.5000% 4.7500%\par 3-Month           2.2500% 2.3750%   2.2500% 2.3750%\par 1-Year            2.2500% 2.3750%   2.2500% 2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010720dx7k000gw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
