\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Higher Late; Yen Gives Region A Lift \b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2001\par 12:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished higher across the board against the U.S. dollar Thursday, enjoying support from a firmer yen. \par \par \uc2  Comments by President George W. Bush that the strong dollar was hurting U.S. exports gave traders an excuse to sell the dollar against the yen in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The comments don't, however, change the consensus view that the dollar will continue to appreciate against the yen as Japan's economy continues to experience difficulties, analysts added. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to Y123.25 late Thursday after trading as high as Y124.40 a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso got an additional boost from a second straight day of suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has suffered from weak economic fundamentals and local corporate demand for dollars. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars in the spot market, but it was denied by monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 53.20 pesos, down from PHP53.74 at the close Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura denied the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Thursday and attributed the peso's gains to new measures that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Department of Finance plan to implement. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar gained on the back of the rising yen. The U.S. dollar closed at NT$35.015, down from Wednesday's close of NT$35.027. \par \par \uc2  Dealers reported some squaring of long U.S. dollar positions in the afternoon session as the yen strengthened. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's main stock index recovered from an intraday decline of 3.7% to finish down just 0.7%. Government funds were suspected of buying shares to support the market. \par \par \uc2  However, pressure to sell the New Taiwan dollar is likely to continue as investor confidence erodes amid Taiwan's worsening economic situation, BA Asia said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to mount a strong defense of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the level of NT$35.2 per U.S. dollar, BA Asia said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar gained ground against the won in early trade, but slipped later as the yen strengthened. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,305.8 won, down from Wednesday's close of KRW1,311.7. \par \par \uc2  A trader at a foreign firm in Seoul said there was heavy dollar-selling from domestic banks at the KRW1,306 level. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah edged higher on guarded optimism that President Abdurrahman Wahid will be ousted. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 11,240 rupiah, down from IDR11,270 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Wahid is viewed by some as a weak and effective leader who is a liability to Indonesia's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The country's highest legislative body has said it plans to impeach Wahid early August, citing his poor leadership and alleged involvement in two financial scandals. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht also moved higher, but corporate demand for dollars and pessimism over the outlook for Thailand's economy supported the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted in late trade at 45.715 baht, down from THB45.790 at the Asian close Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Although the bias in the market remains generally in favor of the dollar, comments from the Bank of Thailand that it was monitoring the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should prevent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising too sharply, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar tracked the yen higher. Late in Asia the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8281, down from S$1.8319 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  around S$1.8300 also supported the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones, Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010719dx7j000my\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
