\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Economy Watch: Singapore Barometer Of Asia's Health \b0\par\par\uc2  By James T. Areddy \par \uc2 811 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2001\par 06:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Singapore is Asia's canary in the coal mine.\par The government's frank economic warnings sent shivers through the region\par but also spurred thinking about finding an escape route.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The Singapore government last week not only announced preliminary figures that suggest it entered a recession for the second time in three years, but officials rubbed salt in the wound by predicting conditions will worsen. \par \par \uc2  "The extent of the slowdown has exceeded what was expected at the beginning of the year, and so has the risk of a more protracted downturn," the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  So much for inspiring confidence. \par \par \uc2  But Singapore's news is important partly for the solutions the government has promised it will apply to relieve the pain, including a dip into its coffers. \par \par \uc2  Asian policy makers have for much of this year done little more than repeat the hopeful wisdom of notables like Federal Reserve Board Chairman Alan Greenspan that the U.S. economy will see growth by late 2001. \par \par \uc2  But it's now apparent Asia's bounce will come later. Singapore's statements last week reinforce views that any recovery will be mild, and will start later than it does in the U.S., requiring official action. \par \par \uc2  The timeliness and reliability of Singapore data help make the bear story a more believable one. Singapore is among the Asian economies most closely tied to the critical electronics cycle, and it will soon be more widely apparent that Asian exporters aren't getting many fresh orders from U.S. purchasing managers. \par \par \uc2  Dong Tao, chief regional economist at Credit Suisse First Boston in Hong Kong, said "in the next three months we're going to see massive bad news from Asian countries." \par \par \uc2  He said markets are prepared for weak - even recessionary gross domestic product - performances. But markets aren't expecting exports in some countries to slide by 25% to 35% from last year, which he predicts. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Many Expect 3Q To Be Asia's Weakest This Year\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fears Asia is returning to the dark days of the 1997 financial crisis\par will undoubtedly rise, and hurt confidence. Many analysts expect the\par third quarter to be Asia's weakest this year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "How you feel about this crisis is going to be very, very bad," Tao\par predicted. "This is going to prompt Asian governments to take action."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In this vein, Singapore also bears watching.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To contrast with its bare-knuckles message last week, the government\par announced proactive ways official Singapore would ease the pain. Its\par steps, to various degrees, are likely to be matched elsewhere in Asia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore's steps included announcing a policy change regarding its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Analysts believe it has tacitly embraced a beggar-thy-neighbor\par strategy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's shift to a "neutral" level on the Singapore dollar\par had the psychological impact of a broker withdrawing a "buy"\par recommendation on a stock. Although the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tried to put a\par floor on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar's fall to\par 11-year lows helped prompt heavy selling of other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par analysts expect weakness to persist as a way to keep export prices as\par attractive as possible.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An alternative way to ease the pain, interest-rate reductions, are a\par policy tool few in Asia have the ability to use. And Singapore\par authorities offered no signals they will ease credit in coming months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Most notably, the Singapore government also said it would announce\par late this month a package of financial "pump priming" measures that it\par hopes will inject some confidence into the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts expect the government - with one eye on a parliamentary\par election due by August 2002 - to bring forward infrastructure works,\par reduce some fees, and possibly slice a bit off the 16% of salaries that\par it mandates employers save for workers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fiscal steps are another area where analysts expect to see other Asian\par governments follow the Singapore example. Indeed, South Korea has also\par announced plans to step up some major construction projects and\par government-linked companies will do the same.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But these won't be economic saviors, so much as politically smart\par moves. "It would be hard to stand idly by with the economy in recession,\par but neither do we think higher government spending will have much\par impact," Deutsche Bank AG economists wrote last week about Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It's got to be remembered that Singapore also has the most ability\par among Southeast Asian countries to dip into its reserves for spending,\par according to Daniel Lian, a Morgan Stanley & Co. economist.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We expect good but not maximum fiscal support from the government,"\par he told investors last week.\par    -James T. Areddy, Dow Jones Newswires, 852.9424.2486,\par james.areddy@dowjones.com\par -0- 17/07/01 04-16G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1172 4255 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010717dx7h00073\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
