\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Yen, weak outlook hurt Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2001\par 10:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 17 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  headed south in late trade on Tuesday as fears of further monetary easing in Japan and the less-than-rosy economic oulook across the region kept the U.S. dollar in favour. \par \par \uc2  The yen came under some pressure when two senior ministers - Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa and Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka - argued there was more that monetary policy could do to support the economy. \par \par \uc2  Their comments reinforced expectations of a further easing from the Bank of Japan which could come at the expense of a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in the region, there was no shortage of bad news. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore, long considered Southeast Asia's oasis, non-oil exports fell a worse than expected 16.9 percent on-year in June. \par \par \uc2  Just a day earlier, Thailand downgraded its export growth target to minus 1.2 to zero percent from a previous forecast of 9.46 percent growth. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar braved through June's dismal non-oil export figures as the market remained on its toes over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said players were reluctant to test 1.8375/80, being mindful of talk that local banks were seeking to offer dollars around 1.8390. Those offers spurred speculation they were acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers added that given the grim outlook for the economy, offshore players were opting to stay long of dollars, and were looking for opportunities to buy on dips. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso hit a fresh six-month trough of 53.95 but avoided a damaging break of 54 thanks to fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers saw a U.S. bank offering around $10 million in the morning at 53.95, spurring talk it was acting on behalf of the authorities which might want to defend the psychological 54 mark. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Rafael Buenaventura said the bank had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday. He added the peso was not totally out of line with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the bank would not adjust interest rates to support it. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar demand from oil companies and manufacturing importers seeking to hedge against further peso weakness was still robust and the peso should breach 54 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah endured a listless session, shackled in a tight range as players focused on rising political tension and developments over the possible release of a frozen $400 million loan from the IMF. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said it was still aiming to release the loan next month, adding a change of government would not delay the disbursement. \par \par \uc2  Legislators said parliamentary debate on controversial \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  law changes had been scheduled to resume early on August 16. The IMF has raised concerns that parts of the proposed law changes could undermine the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's independence. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar lingered around the 35 mark, pressured by a weaker yen and a grim economic outlook but supported by fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not spotted in the market but local corporates and interbank players continued to offload their U.S. dollar longs. \par \par \uc2  Despite modest gains in the local stock market, foreign investors were net sellers of local stocks and settlement of those transactions would continue to pressure the Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht stuck to levels around 45.75 wedged between dollar demand from importers and corporates and trimming of dollar longs by offshore players. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Singapore said U.S. and Singapore names were spotted offering dollars just above 45.80 while local demand was strong around 45.65-45.70. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty over the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest rate policy, fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and of tougher reporting rules has prompted offshore players to book profit on any dollar gains. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said to help hasten economic recovery his government planned to standardise Thai time with regional economic financial centres Singapore and Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  He said the measure was part of a 28-point economic stimulus package approved by his cabinet late Monday. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won was steady in slow, offshore trade. Markets in Seoul were closed for a public holiday, and will re-open on Wednesday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0816 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     125.47       124.88         -0.47\par  Sing dlr      1.8355       1