\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP:Singapore Ctrl Bk Eases Policy In Wake Of Recession \b0\par\par\uc2  By Amit Prakash, Netty Ismail and Hasan Jafri \par \uc2 1,043 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2001\par 09:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Recession-hit Singapore is starting to pull policy levers to soften the blow dealt to its trade-dependent economy by the slowing U.S. economy and, in particular, a sharp drop in global electronics demand. \par \par \uc2  A day after the government promised a package of measures aimed at helping to cut business costs and curb rising unemployment, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore Thursday surprised the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by shifting to a neutral exchange rate policy from a previous tightening stance. \par \par \uc2  It was the first time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased its policy bias since January 2000, when it changed to a policy of allowing a gradual and modest appreciation of the trade weighted Singapore dollar from a policy of allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken during the Asian crisis, MAS officials said. \par \par \uc2  "Against the backdrop of a weaker external economic environment and a more protracted global electronics downturn, near-term growth prospects for the Singapore economy have turned significantly weaker while inflationary pressures are subsiding," Tharman Shanmugaratnam, MAS' managing director said. \par \par \uc2  "MAS has therefore shifted to a neutral exchange rate policy stance, with a policy band centered on a zero percent appreciation" of the trade weighted Singapore dollar, he added. \par \par \uc2  The policy shift took financial markets by surprise, with the Singapore dollar plunging to S$1.8398, yet another 11-year low against the U.S. dollar. However, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  subsequently recovered after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  MAS declined to comment on whether it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Prices of longer term \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds rose as investors switched to safe-haven instruments. \par \par \uc2  However, the stock market took the news in stride, with prices rising marginally partly in response to higher Nasdaq futures. \par \par \uc2  "This (policy shift) caught a lot of us by surprise, but they (MAS) had no choice, the economy was falling off the cliff," said Jimmy Koh, head of treasury research at United Overseas Bank Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Julian Jessop, an economist with Standard Chartered Bank, said the MAS' move was surprising in view of the risk of the knock-on effect that a weaker Singapore dollar could have on regional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. However, the move wasn't "shocking" given the bleak economic data issued this week. \par \par \uc2  At 0634 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8357, up 5.9% from S$1.7340 at the end of 2000. \par \par \uc2  Bhanu Baweja, an economist with research firm IDEAglobal, said he was surprised, "not so much by the policy itself as by the fact it (MAS) is now willing to openly admit it's moving to a neutral to loosening stance." \par \par \uc2  Indeed Singapore's economic prospects are grim. The change comes a day after the government said it's preparing an off-budget package of measures to help businesses and workers ride out the recession. The specific measures will be announced in late July, possibly July 25, when Parliament reconvenes. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, the government issued advance, or flash, estimates showing that Singapore's economy contracted at an annualized pace of 10.1% in the second quarter from the first quarter. Coming after the 11.3% annualized quarter-on-quarter drop in the January-March period, the latest data mean that Singapore is now in recession by conventional standards - two consecutive quarters of GDP shrinkage. \par \par \uc2  "The economy is technically in a recession," the MAS said in its biannual policy statement. \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses the Singapore dollar exchange rate as its main monetary policy tool and, unlike other central banks, doesn't set interest rates which are market determined. Inflation control is the sole declared objective of MAS' monetary policy as Singapore imports almost everything it needs, including water. \par \par \uc2  It manages the Singapore dollar exchange rate against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners and competitors. It maintains the trade-weighted exchange rate within an undisclosed band. \par \par \uc2  Shanmugaratnam said the MAS has switched to a neutral policy stance for the second half of the year, and the trade-weighted Singapore dollar is currently in the "lower half of the new (neutral) band." \par \par \uc2  The MAS' decision to switch to a neutral policy bias from July 1, he said, is consistent with its objective of keeping inflation low in the medium term. He said the MAS usually views the medium term as three years, but sometimes its internal models for the medium-term view run up to five years. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  noted that inflationary pressures are subsiding as oil prices soften. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's consumer price index is expected "to trend down for the rest of the year, coming in within the 1%-1.5% range for 2001 as a whole." \par \par \uc2  Singapore's consumer price index grew at an average of 1.8% from January through May, down from a peak of 2% in the fourth quarter, although the MAS' measure of underlying inflation was still relatively high at 2.3% in the first five months of this year, it said. \par \par \uc2  Shanmugaratnam said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stick to the neutral stance throughout the second half of this year, and review the policy bias at the end of the six-month period. \par \par \uc2  In the light of the policy change, analysts expect the Singapore dollar to gradually weaken to S$1.85 to the U.S. dollar in coming weeks, as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  succumb to a further slowing in their economies. \par \par \uc2  UOB's Jimmy Koh noted that Shanmugaratnam's statement that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in the lower half of the new policy band, implies its downslide will likely be limited for now, "unless you see weakness in regionals, yen or euro." \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Bank strategist Peter Redward said with the recession and falling export and import prices, there is room for the Singapore dollar to fall to S$1.85, as the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls "to the bottom of the trading band in coming weeks." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "By our estimates, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is now just below the middle part of\par the band," he said.\par    -Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 415-4043; amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par    (Bernice Tang and Izham Ahmad contributed to this article)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010712dx7c000ec\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
