\par\par \b \uc2  Sing dlr bounces off lows near 1.84 on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 196 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2001\par 05:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 12 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar bounced back sharply from a fresh 11-year low around 1.84 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, dealers said on Thursday. They said interbank players scrambled to sell the Sing dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) surprised the market by shifting its policy stance to neutral from tightening. \par \par \uc2  "I think it went to 1.84 even and there was some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming in. So the MAS is definitely watchful of the market now. And given the liquidity during lunch-time... they have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1.84," said Thio Chin Loo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  It was quoted at 1.8345/55 at 0352 GMT after hitting lows near 1.84 immediately after the MAS announcement. \par \par \uc2  In its policy review statement, the MAS said inflation pressures were subsiding with the consumer price index to head lower to 1.0-1.5 percent for 2001. The Sing dollar has been under sustained downward pressure since the beginning of the year due to persistent fears of ebbing growth as a slowing global economy eats into exports. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx7c007pu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
