\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late, Off 11-Year Low Vs US Dollar \b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2001\par 11:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was slightly higher against the U.S. dollar late Wednesday after a roller coaster ride, rebounding from 11-year lows. \par \par \uc2  Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and profit-taking helped the local dollar recover from two dips, amid gloomy prospects for the economy in the second half of the year, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0954 GMT (5:54 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8359, down from S$1.8370 late Tuesday. The U.S. dollar traded between S$1.8340 and S$1.8376, a fresh 11-year high. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and drifted higher to a pre-lunch peak of $1.8370, before falling to S$1.8340 on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MAS via its agent banks. \par \par \uc2  In the afternoon, it surged to S$1.8376 after Trade and Industry Minister George Yeo told Parliament that the economy is expected to continue contracting in the third quarter, and that overall growth in the second half is likely to be flat from a year ago. \par \par \uc2  Yeo and Manpower Minister Lee Boon Yang said unemployment would worsen in the second half of this year, and that around 20,000 job losses are expected in 2001. \par \par \uc2  The government will announce a package of measured aimed at softening the blow dealt to employers and workers alike by the current recession, Yeo said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar retreated from its 11-year peak on profit-taking by market participants. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, however, market participants are looking to the MAS' biannual policy review Thursday to provide direction to the market for the near term. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to smoothen volatility, and expect the U.S. dollar's upside to be capped between S$1.8375 and S$1.8380 overnight, with the next resistance level at S$1.8400. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar expected to find support S$1.8330. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore government bonds ended higher, amid bleak prospects for the local economy this year, and on tumbling equity prices in both the U.S. and Singapore, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds ended down two basis points at 2.16%, while the yield on five-year bonds fell one basis point to 2.92%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yields on seven-year and 10-year bonds were down two basis points each at 3.30% and 3.59%, respectively. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and\par are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be\par viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:\par      \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps ended mostly lower, tracking\par local bond yields.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer closed two basis points lower at\par 3.48%, while the 10-year swap offer also ended two basis points lower at\par 3.72%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offers was unchanged at\par 106 basis points from late Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interbank money market, rates were mostly lower amid flush\par liquidity, except the one-year rate, which remained flat.\par                   Late Wednesday    Late Tuesday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        1.5625%  1.6875%   1.5000%  1.7500%\par 3-Month          2.0000%  2.1250%   2.0625%  2.1875%\par 1-Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4157;\par izham.ahmad@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx7b000lk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
