\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late In Thin Range-Bound Mkt; Tracks Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2001\par 10:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher against the U.S. dollar late Tuesday, as it tracked a slightly stronger yen in thin trading, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0759 GMT (3:59 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8212, down from S$1.8229 late Monday. It traded between S$1.8212 and S$1.8225. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar opened around S$1.8225 to the U.S. dollar and drifted higher as it tracked the yen's rise from Y124.15 to around Y123.90. \par \par \uc2  Japanese and local banks were among the main sellers of U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars, a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was generally quiet as participants focused on their positions in other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It (Singapore dollar) has been stuck in (the S$1.8200-30) range for the past week," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore unit is expected to find support around S$1.8240 amid\par fears that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  below that level, with buy orders from companies and U.S.\par names forming resistance around 1.8200, dealers said.\par - - 03/07/01 08-30G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Prices of benchmark Singapore government bonds closed slightly higher\par Tuesday, tracking U.S. Treasurys, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There was some buying as the bonds were starting to look oversold\par from the past two sessions," a dealer at a local bank said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "But we won't be seeing any sort of big rally as the market is still\par watching out for key U.S. economic figures later tonight and this\par Friday," the dealer added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on two-year bonds was three basis points lower at 2.20%,\par while the five-year bond yield ended down two basis point at 2.94%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the longer end of the curve, the yield on seven-year bonds was down\par two basis points at 3.32%, while the 10-year bond yield finished one\par basis point lower at 3.63%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and\par are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be\par viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web Site:\par \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps ended flat to a tad lower, in\par tandem with local bond yields and overnight U.S. swap rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interbank money market, interest rates held mostly steady.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer closed one basis point down at\par 3.53%, while the 10-year swap offer also ended one basis point lower at\par 3.77%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer was unchanged from\par Monday at 108 basis points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Tuesday       Late Monday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        1.8750%  2.0000%   1.7500%  2.0000%\par 3 Month          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par -By