\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Singapore Dlr Falls As Data Signal Recession \b0\par\par\uc2  By Amit Prakash \par \uc2 836 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2001\par 08:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Mounting evidence that Singapore's economy is\par heading into a recession hurt the Singapore dollar Wednesday, and\par analysts expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken further before recovering by the\par end of this year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar fell to S$1.8213 to the U.S. dollar, from S$1.8174 late Tuesday on data showing that Singapore's industrial output fell 10.9% on year in May. The fall was much sharper than a revised 0.7% contraction in April, and steeper than economists' forecasts of a 6% to 8% contraction in May. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the data point to a high probability that Singapore's gross domestic product will contract in the second quarter from the first quarter, following a 11.3% quarter-on-quarter contraction in the first quarter. This would put the economy in a recession, defined as a quarter-on-quarter contraction in two consecutive quarters. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also hurt by a weaker yen and by reported comments from National Trade Union Congress chief Lim Boon Heng, that Singaporeans must brace for a longer-than-expected economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  Hui Chin Chong, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with JP Morgan Chase, said she expects the Singapore dollar to fall to S$1.8300 by end-July before recovering to S$1.8000 by year- end, largely on the back of an expected recovery in the euro and yen later this year. \par \par \uc2  In the near-term, apart from a sharp slowdown in the local and global economies, the Singapore dollar will remain pressured by an estimated US$9.5 billion in outflows arising from overseas acquisitions by Singapore companies, Chong said. \par \par \uc2  Singapore Telecommunication's Ltd.'s (P.SGT) bid for Australia's Cable & Wireless Optus (A.CWO) and DBS Group Holdings Ltd.'s (P.DBS) bid for Hong Kong's Dao Heng Bank account for the bulk of these outflows. \par \par \uc2  Standard Chartered Bank's three-month forecast for the Singapore dollar stands at S$1.8400, and it too expects a recovery to S$1.8000 by year-end. Research form IDEAglobal expects the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken to S$1.8300 in the third quarter. \par \par \uc2  At 0529 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8210, with reports of a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  capping its upside at S$1.8220. \par \par \uc2  The latest data and Lim's comments come on the heels of last week's news of a sharper-than-expected 9% year-on-year contraction in Singapore's key non-oil domestic exports, due to a 10.9% fall in electronics exports and a 5.9% decline in the exports of non-electronic products. \par \par \uc2  The electronics sector output in May plunged by 20.1% from a year ago as a result of the slowdown in the U.S. economy and reduced global appetite for electronic products, Singapore's leading export. In April, electronics production fell 8.5% from a year ago. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect industrial production and non-oil exports to fall further in June following a spate of profit warnings by local and U.S. technology companies, and with no apparent signs of a turnaround in growth. \par \par \uc2  "Industrial production is expected to continue to worsen in June, meaning that second quarter GDP (year-on-year) growth" will slow to 2% from 4.5% in the first quarter, IDEAglobal said in a research note, adding that the May manufacturing performance was Singapore's worst in 10 years. \par \par \uc2  "This would bring the economy very close to, if not already into a... recession, which would occur if growth (slows) to 1.8% on year in the second quarter," IDEAglobal added. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's DBS Bank said it expects GDP to grow by a mere 1% to 1.5% year-on-year in the second quarter, suggesting a recession. \par \par \uc2  Standard Chartered Bank strategist Julian Jessop said recession appears inevitable as his bank expects the second quarter GDP to contract 0.7% from the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect the government to lower its current 3.5% to 5.5% GDP forecast for 2001 when it unveils its advance estimate for second-quarter GDP growth on July 10. The advance, or flash, estimate is based on data from the first two months of the quarter. \par \par \uc2  The expected revised forecast range is 2.5%-5%. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say that while the data should prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore to ease its monetary policy bias from moderately tight to neutral, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not want to do so publicly. This would likely result in a reaffirmation of its policy stance at the next scheduled review in July. \par \par \uc2  "If they haven't changed it (policy) already, I'm not convinced they'll do it in the middle of the year," Jessop said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 27/06/01 08-11G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx6r02v7t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
