\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late; US Dlr Sold On MAS Action Talk \b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2001\par 10:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher late Monday, on light U.S. dollar selling by speculators following market talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's agents likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday. \par \par \uc2  Around 0801 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8198, down from S$1.8211 late Friday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower, around S$1.8206 and fell to S$1.8188 on selling by some speculators and a U.K. based bank. Later in the day, some U.S. banks bought U.S. dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The range has been tight and people are playing it safe given the market rumors of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MAS (\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) on Friday," a dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The MAS declined to confirm or deny its actions, saying it normally doesn't comment on its market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that the U.S. dollar's retreat will likely be short-lived, and that market participants are expected to continue buying U.S. dollars on dips in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "Right now the MAS fear is very much there, so nobody is taking much interest in the Sing," a second dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to remain supported at S$1.8180 with initial resistance at S$1.8220 and then at S$1.8250. \par \par \uc2  Prices of benchmark Singapore government bonds ended flat to slightly lower in a quiet market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect trading to pick up later this week with the auction of a S$2.3 billion 10-year government bond by MAS, the launch of bond futures on the Singapore Exchange, and the Federal Open Market Committee meeting. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds ended five basis points higher at 2.21%, while the five-year bond yield rose one basis point to 2.92%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the longer end of the curve, the yields on the seven- and 10-year\par bonds finished flat at 3.30% and 3.57%, respectively.\par    The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and\par are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be\par viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web Site:\par    \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps ended lower.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interbank money market, term interest rates were mostly steady.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer closed two basis points lower at\par 3.49%, and the 10-year swap offer ended one basis point lower at 3.73%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer narrowed to 107\par basis points from 108 basis points Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Monday        Late Friday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        2.1250%  2.2500%   2.5000%  2.6250%\par 3 Month          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx6p02uhv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
