\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Down Late, But Off Low On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Talk \b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2001\par 10:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was lower late Thursday, tracking a weaker yen, but was off its intraday low amid market speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smoothen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0822 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8208, up from S$1.8171 late Wednesday in Asia. It traded between S$1.8202 and S$1.8236. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened higher, tracking its overnight rise above S$1.8200 in line with its strong gains against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar hit a high of S$1.8236 in line with its rise above Y124, but retreated amid suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The usual agents of the MAS were seen selling (U.S. dollars) around S$1.8235, but its wasn't strong or aggressive suggesting that they wanted to smoothen the rise and not reverse it," said a senior dealer at a U.S. brokerage in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "The market is worried about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but so far nothing is confirmed," a dealer at a European bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that a U.K.-based bank sold U.S. dollars in the afternoon, pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below S$1.8210. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to remain supported overnight at S$1.8180, with resistance at S$1.8140 - the MAS' last suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level. \par \par \uc2  However, market participants expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar to continue to weaken further, especially if the yen continued to decline and the MAS decides to heed analysts call for an easing of its exchange rate policy at its scheduled review in July. \par \par \uc2  Prices of benchmark Singapore government bonds ended higher on continued buying and in line with higher U.S. Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two-year bonds ended two basis point lower at 2.22%, while the five-year bond yield fell three basis points to 2.92%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yields on the seven- and 10 year bonds finished one basis point lower at 3.31% and 3.58%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, swaps tracked bond yields lower.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interbank money market, term interest rates were mostly steady.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer closed one basis point lower at\par 3.51%, and the 10-year swap offer also ended one basis point lower at\par 3.75%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer narrowed to 107\par basis points from 108 basis points Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Thursday      Late Wednesday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        2.3750%  2.5000%   1.8750%  2.1250%\par 3 Month          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx6l02v7r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
