\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Fretting over yen, US outlook. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,175 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2001\par 03:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 18 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  headed south early Monday as the yen weakened and U.S data over the weekend showed the economy was far from a recovery. \par \par \uc2  The data and more corporate profit warnings depressed Wall Street, which will impact the region's tech-sensitive markets. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq ended down for the sixth straight session finishing the week with an 8.4 percent loss, its biggest weekly decline this year. \par \par \uc2  The yen was wobbling near the 123 mark in early Tokyo trade, after a decline to three-week lows against the dollar in New York on Friday with the focus on Japan's poor economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  Both Japan and the U.S. are Asia's main export markets. \par \par \uc2  The regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can expect little help from domestic factors with Singapore likely to announce weak May non-oil domestic export data due to worsening global technology demand. Thailand's state think tank announced first quarter GDP growth of 1.8 percent, below analysts' already bearish forecasts of 1.94 percent growth. \par \par \uc2  Markets are also eagerly awaiting Thai Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra's speech in the constitutional court to defend himself against allegations he deliberately concealed some of his wealth when he was deputy prime minister in the late '90s. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht held below 45.30, within its week-old range as investors eyed Thaksin's speech due around 0630 GMT, at the close of his graft trial. \par \par \uc2  A verdict is not expected until July or August. If found guilty of deliberately concealing his assets, he will be banned from politics for five years. \par \par \uc2  Thaksin told reporters on Saturday he was not considering fixing the weakening baht to the dollar, denying remarks from a leading economist that \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy amounted to a virtual peg. \par \par \uc2  Ammar Siamwala, economist at a private think-tank, told a local newspaper the government had an ideal level in mind for the baht, as evidenced by the fact the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had both bought and sold dollars on several occasions since the new governor was appointed two weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, uncertainty persisted over the Bank of Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, with the bank saying it had not abandonned efforts to curb speculation by non-residents, but wanted to hear the views of commercial banks before introducing further controls. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won touched an early low of 1,303, last seen on May 21, on worries over the direction of the yen and U.S. tech stocks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Singapore said players scrambled to beef up their dollar longs as Korean stocks got off to a weak start. \par \par \uc2  They said most interbank players had recently trimmed their dollar longs as the won, helped by a steadier yen, equity-related inflows and talk of foreign direct investment deals, had managed to stay above the 1,300 level since May 29. \par \par \uc2  However, hopes General Motors would soon reach a deal to take over Daewoo Motor and expected foreign investments in Hyundai Investment & Trust Securities and Hynix Semiconductor will keep the won's downside limited. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah retained its bearish tone but was well within its two-week-old 11,000-11270 range as a fuel price hike and ensuing violent protests upstaged news the World Bank was set to approve a $320 million loan to Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Thousands of protesters rallied in the city of Bandung on Saturday and hundreds of bus drivers went on strike, angered by the government's decision to push ahead with a 30.1 percent fuel price hike. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia is Asia's only OPEC member. It had some of the world's lowest fuel prices but at a huge cost in subsidies which the financially ailing government can no longer afford. \par \par \uc2  The World Bank said on Sunday it would approve a $320 million community development loan for Indonesia later this month as part of its $1.2 billion, three-year aid programme. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR The Sing dollar stumbled to 1.8178 from 1.8105 late on Friday, tracking the lower yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said U.S. names were bidding for dollar/Sing but fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities encouraged them to take profits at 1.8180. \par \par \uc2  Any dips in the dollar were expected to be met by fresh bids by European banks at around 1.8130 as the market awaited trade data, due at 0430 GMT. Non-oil exports fell 1.2 percent on-year in April. PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso buckled to 51.75, its lowest since January 19, compared with 51.59 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Manila dealers said the falling yen and the unsolved kidnapping saga in the southern Philippines encouraged foreign banks to bid aggressively from 51.60. \par \par \uc2  Officials said on Sunday they feared Muslim rebels had killed one of three American hostages they are holding along with more than 20 Filipinos since May 27. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might emerge around 52, but players were looking to take profits at around 51.75 given a 14-day relative strength index reading showing the dollar was overbought at 81.19. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Rafael Buenaventura said on Sunday there was little room left for aggressive rate cuts due to the weakness of the peso and inflation pressures. \par \par \uc2  Socio-economic Planning Secretary Dante Canlas said he expected annual inflation in June to rise slightly to 6.7 to 6.8 percent from 6.5 percent in May due to higher consumer spending related to the start of the school year. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Pressured by a softer yen and worries over the tech sector's second quarter earnings, the Taiwan dollar touched a low of 34.43 early, where \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted trimming of U.S dollar longs. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said players were still seeking U.S. dollars and a test of 34.50, last seen on May 28, was possible if the yen weakened further later in the day. \par \par \uc2  They said second quarter profit warnings by tech companies in the U.S. and at home would further dampen sentiment already depressed by poor economic data for the first quarter. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0145 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     122.88\tab  121.37\tab    -1.23\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8160\tab  1.8105\tab    -0.30\par  Taiwan dlr    34.362\tab  34.370\tab    +0.02\par  Korean won   1295.00\tab 1291.00\tab    -0.31\par  Baht\tab      45.33\tab   45.27\tab    -0.13\par  Peso\tab      51.71\tab   51.59\tab    -0.23\par  Rupiah\tab 11280.00     11250.00\tab    -0.27\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7999\tab  3.7995\tab    -0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     122.88\tab  114.45\tab    -6.86\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8160\tab  1.7365\tab    -4.38\par  Taiwan dlr    34.362\tab  32.900\tab    -4.25\par  Korean w