\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Yen helps, but U.S. data worries. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,088 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2001\par 04:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 14 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held their recent ranges early Thursday, helped by a steadier yen but warily eyeing U.S. data released overnight that showed the global growth engine was still sputtering badly. \par \par \uc2  U.S. retail sales, closely monitored as a proxy for overall consumer spending, nudged up just 0.1 in May percent after an upwardly revised 1.4 percent gain in April. \par \par \uc2  Consumer spending, which fuels two-thirds of economic activity is threatened by increasing layoffs by U.S. companies. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve's monthly Beige Book showed economic activity little changed or slower in April with weak manufacturing, and layoffs increasing. \par \par \uc2  Taking advantage of the bearish U.S. data, the yen recovered from its three-week lows and the euro reached a one-week high also aided by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears. \par \par \uc2  But dealers doubted the yen would make further gains ahead of the Bank of Japan's two-day policy meeting starting on Thursday. The BOJ is facing political pressure to take further easing steps to avoid a recession. \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said on Thursday he saw no need for new monetary policy steps as the recent fall in stock prices and slowdown in output was within expectations. \par \par \uc2  For more evidence of U.S. sluggishness, markets will watch out for the May Producer Price Index, first-time claims for jobless benefits for the week ended June 9 and business inventories for April, due later Thursday. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar opened higher at 34.274 on the back of a steadier yen before market bearishness dragged it lower again. \par \par \uc2  "With a foreign bank calling for 36.00 for the Taiwan dollar and offshore funds speculating that the authorities will engineer a one-time devaluation to 40.00, the onshore market panicked yesterday," said a UBS Warburg note. \par \par \uc2  "And with U.S. dollar demand remaining good, the market is likely to keep dollar longs and will now target 35.00," it said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market on Wednesday to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling the U.S. dollar as it reached 2-= week lows, but its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sporadic. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was stuck just above 1.8150 after hitting a month low of 1.8180 on Wednesday where players unwound U.S. dollars on fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said rumours Canadian Celestica Inc was making a takeover bid for local electronic contract manufacturer Omni Industries could support the Singapore dollar and they predicted a range of 1.8150-1.8180 for the day. \par \par \uc2  But overall, players were looking to snap up U.S. dollars on dips given a cloudy growth and export outlook. The market will also stay cautious ahead of April retail sales data due Friday. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah held steady around 11,250, extending the past week's consolidation, as corporates stayed out and offshore and interbank players trimmed dollar longs on a growing perception embattled President Abdurrahman Wahid would step down peacefully. \par \par \uc2  It was also supported by expectations parliament would approve a revised budget and hopes an IMF team would visit Jakarta to review progress on economic reforms next week. \par \par \uc2  In a fresh sign of mounting political pressure on Wahid, his deputy snubbed the inauguration ceremony for new ministers appointed after the latest cabinet reshuffle. \par \par \uc2  The new chief economics minister said on Wednesday the rupiah was undervalued and should be in a 9,000-10,000 range. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah has crept higher against the dollar since it touched a low of 12,215 on April 26. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht was wedged below 45.30 as players unwound some dollar longs after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it had scrapped a plan to impose tighter reporting guidelines on non-resident trading in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Prior to the announcement, commercial banks had complained that an official form the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  required them to fill out for this purpose was complicated and not practical. \par \par \uc2  However, the baht's downside was supported by rising funding costs and news the government asset management company would be up and running in July to tackle Thailand's bad asset problems. \par \par \uc2  In the offshore market, the one-month forward rate rose to 10/15 satang per dollar in early trade while the 12-month tenor remained firm at 105/120 satang per dollar. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso was little moved from Wednesday's onshore close of 51.31 and dealers expected a narrow range of 51.20-51.40. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Manila said worries about the fate of Americans kidnapped by a Muslim rebel group and concerns about growth and export prospects would keep underlying sentiment bearish. \par \par \uc2  But a lack of dollar demand from corporates and importers and trimming of stale dollar longs by interbank players were likely to limit its downside. \par \par \uc2  President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo said on Wednesday the government had lowered its 2001 gross domestic product growth target to 3.3-3.8 percent from 3.8-4.3 percent, partly due to the impact of kidnappings in the rebel-infested southern islands. KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won opened a touch firmer after the yen recouped some lost ground and a nationwide labour union strike lost steam as workers at Korean Air and hospitals returned to work. \par \par \uc2  The won has been in an uptrend since early April on expectations of foreign direct investments. \par \par \uc2  But with the yen weakness expected to continue, UBS Warburg said offshore players would be encouraged to buy the dollar and it expected the next target to be 1,320. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0211 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     121.80\tab  122.05\tab    +0.21\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8162\tab  1.8157\tab    -0.03\par  Taiwan dlr    34.421\tab  34.377\tab    -0.13\par  Korean won   1291.00\tab 1293.30\tab    +0.18\par  Baht\tab      45.32\tab   45.33\tab    +0.03\par  Peso\tab      51.32\tab   51.31\tab    -0.02\par  Rupiah\tab 11245.00     11220.00\tab    -0.22\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     121.80\tab  114.45\tab    -6.03\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8162\tab  1.7365\tab    -4.39\par  Taiwan dlr    34.421\tab  32.900\tab    -4.42\par  Korean won   1291.00\tab 1277.00\tab    -1.08\par  Baht\tab      45.32\tab   43.70\tab    -3.56\par  Peso\tab      51.32\tab   50.75\tab    -1.11\par  Rupiah\tab 11245.00\tab 9470.00\tab   -15.78\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6e01k04\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw18