\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Down Late; Tracks Weaker Yen In Light Trade \b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2001\par 10:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower late Tuesday, tracking a weaker yen in relatively light trading, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0832 GMT (4:32 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8146, up from S$1.8119 late Monday in Asia. It traded in a narrow range between S$1.8133 and S$1.8155. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened higher in line with its gains against the yen. But the dollars gains against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were limited by lack of trading interest and general caution against the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market was quite thin today with no interbank call being made. The business was largely through brokers," said a senior dealer at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "The last...sale of (U.S. dollars) by MAS occurred at 1.8140/50, and so some players may be nervous at these levels," Bank of America said in an afternoon research note. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers expect MAS fears to cap the U.S. dollar's upside at S$1.8180,\par with support at S$1.8100 overnight.\par - - 12/06/01 08-46G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Prices of benchmark Singapore government bonds were flat Tuesday in a\par quiet market, with trading confined to three-month and one-year treasury\par bills ahead of the maturity Friday of S$1 billion worth of seven-year\par government bonds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said there was negligible, if any, activity in benchmark bonds\par of two- to 10-year maturity. Bond trading is expected to remain quiet\par over the next couple of weeks as participants save their appetite for an\par auction of 10-year bonds scheduled to be held in the last week of June.\par The bonds are to be issued to successful bidders July 2.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Interest rate swaps were slightly higher in the wake of a weaker\par Singapore dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interbank money market, term interest rates remained steady,\par while the overnight rate stayed below 2% amid ample liquidity in the\par banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on two- and five-year bonds ended flat at 2.26% and 2.94%,\par respectively.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the longer end of the curve, the yields on the seven- and 10-year\par bonds also finished flat at 2.29% and 3.55%, respectively.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and\par are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be\par viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web Site:\par \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer closed one basis point higher at\par 3.52%, while the 10-year swap offer rose one basis point to 3.74%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer widened to 106\par basis points from 105 basis points Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Tuesday       Late Monday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        1.5000%  1.6250%   1.6250%  1.6875%\par 3 Month          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par