\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Yen helps, but local news hinders. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,061 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2001\par 10:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 29 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held their ground with the help of the steadier yen late Tuesday, but local politics and poor economic data threatened to topple them out of their recent ranges. \par \par \uc2  Korea received confirmation of its slowdown with April industrial output falling by 1.3 percent on month against expectations of a 0.6 percent decline. \par \par \uc2  Politics continued to dominate Southeast Asian markets, with the Indonesian rupiah nervously eyeing Wednesday's key parliamentary session, which is expected to bring President Abdurrahman Wahid one step closer to impeachment. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the baht see-sawed when the cabinet announced the sacking of Bank of Thailand Governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul, who had been engaged in a row with Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra over low interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The yen was steady against the dollar with investors eyeing London and New York trade for direction. Both markets were closed on Monday for public holidays. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht bounced up to around 45.50 after a dip following the government's decision to sack the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor. \par \par \uc2  It touched a session low of 45.64 as soon as the news broke, mainly due to one U.S. investment bank covering short dollar positions taken earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  But soon the market started to price in a rise in baht funding costs as interbank money market rates rose in anticipation of a hike in key rates and some state-linked companies were seen selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  Chatu Mongol was replaced by Pridiyathorn Devakula, chief of Thailand's Import-Export Bank and a leading supporter of the argument that higher rates were needed to kick-start the economy. \par \par \uc2  However, government officials were quick to deny talk of rate hikes saying the change of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief would not affect monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The incoming governor said the market should decide the appropriate level of interest rates, adding that the bank would ensure Thai monetary and fiscal policy were in line, to kick-start the economy. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah recovered to around 11,300 from lows of about 11,620 thanks to subdued corporate dollar demand and profit-taking in a market heavily long of dollars. \par \par \uc2  However, the relative calm could soon be disrupted, with parliament poised to decide on a special legislative session to impeach Wahid, who has been censured twice over charges of corruption and incompetency. \par \par \uc2  Wahid and other government officials have warned of violence if efforts to impeach him move ahead. Most protests so far have been conducted by Wahid's supporters. \par \par \uc2  Charts showed the dollar/rupiah has been on a downward trend since February 26, with support around 11,150. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won was holding up near its session highs around 1,290 as month-end dollar sales by exporters trickled in through the day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar sales also helped the won off a low of 1,300 touched after industrial output data came in worse than expected and foreign funds, which had been selling local stocks for the past several sessions, came in to buy dollars. \par \par \uc2  April output fell a seasonally adjusted 1.3 percent month-on-month, against a 0.4 percent rise in March as exports limped and domestic consumption came off. \par \par \uc2  The April current account surplus fell to $634.8 million from $1.81 billion in March largely due to a 9.9 percent year-on-year fall in exports. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar recovered a bit as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted profit-taking on the dollar's three percent rise on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Singapore dealers said offshore players took profit on their long dollar positions in early trade, and were waiting for the market to stabilise before taking on fresh dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  "The market really overshot itself on Monday," said a European bank dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Charts showed the dollar/Taiwan was venturing back from extremely overbought territory, with a 14-day relative strength index reading of around 73 against Monday's 98. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers said the Taiwan dollar's gains were likely to be capped just below the 34 level as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is viewed as tolerating a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its bid to boost Taiwan's flagging economy. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR The Sing dollar clambered back to around 1.8100 having hit lows of 1.8150 earlier, as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  benefited from the firmer yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said local banks which bought the U.S. dollar earlier began selling at around 1.8150, raising worries about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They added some interbank players were keen to buy the U.S. dollar on dips around 1.8080, limiting the Sing dollar's upside. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso recouped losses to 50.53 from early lows of 50.88. \par \par \uc2  Manila dealers said players took profit on their long dollar positions at 50.65-70 as the yen firmed, but car importers and foreign banks were looking to buy the dollar on dips at 50.30-50. \par \par \uc2  The market kept a wary eye on the fate of 20 hostages held by Muslim rebels since Sunday in the southern Philippines. \par \par \uc2  Local radio reports said the rebels threatened to kill their captives if they came under military attack. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also eyed a special Congressional session to pass a key power reform bill, seen as a litmus test of President Gloria Arroyo's commitment to economic reforms. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0819 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     120.37\tab  121.22\tab    +0.71\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8087\tab  1.8127\tab    +0.22\par  Taiwan dlr    34.072\tab  34.500\tab    +1.26\par  Korean won   1289.00\tab 1295.00\tab    +0.47\par  Baht\tab      45.45\tab   45.57\tab    +0.26\par  Peso\tab      50.49\tab   50.77\tab    +0.55\par  Rupiah\tab 11280.00     11590.00\tab    +2.75\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     120.37\tab  114.45\tab    -4.92\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8087\tab  1.7365\tab    -3.99\par  Taiwan dlr    34.072\tab  32.900\tab    -3.44\par  Korean won   1289.00\tab 1277.00\tab    -0.93\par  Baht\tab      45.45\tab   43.70\tab    -3.85\par  Peso\tab      50.49\tab   50.75\tab    +0.51\par  Rupiah\tab 11280.00\tab 9470.00\tab   -16.05\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5t02rds\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
