\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Treading water, Japan data eyed. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,060 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2001\par 03:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 28 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled back early Monday as fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took away the yen's gains in a market thinned by holidays in London and New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro got a new lease on life as talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept investors on alert after it hovered just above six-month lows against the dollar earlier last week. \par \par \uc2  Gains in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week were mostly due to the yen's rise, which was propelled by the euro's weakness rather than any improvement in Japan's economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The market was focused on Japan's April industrial output due at 2350 GMT Monday. Economists polled by Reuters expected the data to show a fall for the second straight month. Elsewhere, local politics in Bangkok and Jakarta clouded the Thai baht and the Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Investors were likely to stick to long dollar/baht positions as the Constitutional Court holds hearings on whether to uphold a ruling that Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra failed to correctly declare his assets during the 1990s. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah retained its bearish tone ahead of Wednesday's special parliamentary meeting, which could set in motion President Abdurrahman Wahid's impeachment. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar edged down to 1.8080 as players bought the dollar after disappointing output data released at the weekend. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's manufacturing output dipped 0.8 percent in April from a year ago after rising 6.4 percent in March, though it rose 6.1 percent on a three-month moving average. \par \par \uc2  Electronics output fell 8.8 percent in April from a year ago, on top of a five percent fall in March. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said local banks were keen to buy the Sing dollar at 1.8100, 1.8130 and below, reminding the market of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The MAS admitted earlier this month it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the Sing dollar. Dealers expected it to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the Sing dollar breached 1.8150. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso slipped to 50.80 from 50.50 late on Friday in extremely thin morning trading. \par \par \uc2  "You want to buy the dollar, but you can't because no one is offering," a Manila dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Investors were upset by news that gunmen believed to be Muslim rebels seized 20 people including two U.S. missionaries on Sunday on a tourist resort in the the southern Philippines. \par \par \uc2  Reflecting jitters and underlying political uncertainty, four foreign brokerage houses have either ceased or suspended \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Philippines this year. More are expected to follow suit. \par \par \uc2  Socio-economic Planning Secretary dante Canlas said late on Friday he expected annual inflation to remain flat in May, and economic growth in the first quarter should be around 2.3-3.0 percent. The government is due to release inflation data for May in two weeks. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar edged closer towards the 33.50 mark, depressed over much lower than expected first quarter gross domestic product growth. \par \par \uc2  After the market's close on Friday, the state statistics agency said Taiwan saw growth of only 1.06 percent on year, against forecasts for 3.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  The government also lowered its GDP target for 2001 to 4.02 percent from a previous forecast of 5.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah headed south in thin early trade, hitting one-month lows, with little relief in sight ahead of Wednesday's parliamentary meeting. \par \par \uc2  Parliament is expected to decide on whether to take the next step towards impeaching President Abdurrahman Wahid and call upon a special session of the top legislative body. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, a presidential spokesperson said Wahid, who was censured twice by parliament, was considering a state of civil emergency if parliament pressed ahead with impeachment. \par \par \uc2  However, it is unclear whether his senior ministers or the military woud support such a move. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won edged down on a softer opening of the local stock market but dealers in Singapore said the downside should be limited by continued equity-linked inflows and General Motors' expected takeover bid for Daewoo Motor. \par \par \uc2  They said Korean media reports suggested a GM delegation would reach Seoul on Monday and might announce the bid soon. \par \par \uc2  However, players were likely to tread cautiously amid uncertainty about the direction of the yen and ahead of some key economic data due this week. \par \par \uc2  April industrial output and current account data are due on Tuesday, the May producer price index on Thursday and May trade figures on Friday. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht pulled back below a key psychological level of 45.50 as importers and interbank players bought the dollar in the absence of fresh baht-supportive leads. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Singapore said players were reluctant to buy the baht as it was not clear what the outcome of the court hearing on Prime Minister Thaksin would be even though some analysts believed his chances of surviving the case and holding on to his job now looked brighter. \par \par \uc2  Charts showed the dollar/baht was still trapped in a protracted consolidative phase which started around April 10. \par \par \uc2  The market is also focused on further development of the state-run Thai Asset Management Corp (TAMC) which will buy upto 1.2 trillion baht worth of bad loans from banks. \par \par \uc2  It is expected to get the formal green light this week and to start \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0153 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     120.56\tab  120.58\tab    +0.02\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8098\tab  1.8063\tab    -0.19\par  Taiwan dlr    33.495\tab  33.495\tab    +0.00\par  Korean won   1288.90\tab 1288.90\tab    +0.00\par  Baht\tab      45.59\tab   45.51\tab    -0.18\par  Peso\tab      50.85\tab   50.51\tab    -0.68\par  Rupiah\tab 11630.00     11440.00\tab    -1.63\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     120.56\tab  114.45\tab    -5.07\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8098\tab  1.7365\tab    -4.05\par  Taiwan dlr    33.495\tab  32.900\tab    -1.78\par  Korean won   1288.90\tab 1277.00\tab    -0.92\par  Baht\tab      45.59\tab   43.70\tab    -4.15\par  Peso\tab      50.85\tab   50.75\tab    -0.20\par  Rupiah\tab 11630.00\tab 9470.00\tab   -18.57\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5s02xna\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
