\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Lower Late; Tracks Yen, Regionals \b0\par\par\uc2 532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2001\par 10:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower late Monday, tracking weaker regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the yen, dealers said. However, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears capped the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's downside. \par \par \uc2  Around 0816 GMT (4:16 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8115, up from S$1.8058 late Friday in Asia. It traded between S$1.8080 and S$1.8123 Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened higher, following late Friday's gains in London and New York. After a brief dip early in the day, it resumed its ascent across Asia, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said European and U.S. banks and investment houses were the main buyers of U.S. dollars Monday, while local banks acting on customer orders and one U.S. bank were the main sellers. \par \par \uc2  Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep inflation in check prevented market participants from testing the immediate resistance at S$1.8130. \par \par \uc2  With April inflation at the top of the 1-2% tolerance band, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to resume \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the U.S. dollar's moves toward S$1.8200. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar remain well supported at S$1.8080, dealers added. \par \par \uc2  Prices of benchmark Singapore government bonds ended mostly steady to a touch lower in thin trading. \par \par \uc2  Bond prices have held steady for the past two weeks as participants are comfortable with their positions and expect negligible downside to domestic interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "Prices were quoted slightly lower because of the higher rates, but hardly one or two trades were done today," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, the overnight rate eased from Friday's 3%, but remained above 2% for most of the day due to a weaker Singapore dollar. Term rates edged higher for the same reason. \par \par \uc2  Interest rate swaps ended narrowly mixed in a very thin market, with shorter-dated swaps gaining the most, while the seven- and 10-year swaps eased a touch. \par \par \uc2  The yield on two- and five-year bonds ended flat at 2.29% and 2.96%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on the seven-year bond was flat at 3.33%, while the yield on the 10-year bond rose one basis point to end 3.56%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer ended one basis point lower at\par 3.54%, and the 10-year swap offer also fell one basis point, to 3.76%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer narrowed to 103\par basis points from 106 basis points late Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Monday        Late Friday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        2.0000%  2.2500%   2.7500%  2.8750%\par 3 Month          2.1875%  2.3125%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.3125%  2.4375%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \