\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late, But Off Low; Local Bks Buy US Dlrs \b0\par\par\uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2001\par 10:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower against the U.S. dollar late Friday, but off its intraday low, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0834 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8058, up from S$1.8029 late Thursday in Asia. It traded between S$1.8020 and S$1.8080. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened higher, tracking its gains against the yen, and rose further on buying by Singapore banks to fulfill corporate orders, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated after U.S. banks and investors sold long-U.S. dollar positions. A large local bank also joined in the selling to cut losses on the significant long positions it had built earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  "Around S$1.8060, a big European bank also joined the selling and pushed (the U.S. dollar) into the S$1.8040s (by late afternoon)," said a senior dealer at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar's gains, for now, are expected to be capped at S$1.8080 on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears. \par \par \uc2  In a research note, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's DBS Bank said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further to defend the local dollar if inflation continues to trend higher following a 2% rise in Singapore's April consumer price index. \par \par \uc2  April inflation is at the top of the MAS' 1%-2% tolerance band. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that, while in the short term, renewed yen strength could push the U.S. dollar lower against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely find support from local companies around S$1.8000. \par \par \uc2  Prices of benchmark Singapore government bonds ended mostly steady to a touch lower in thin trading. Bond prices have held steady since last week as participants are comfortable with their positions and expect negligible downside to domestic interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Interest rate swaps ended higher, tracking a weaker local dollar. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, the overnight rate rose above 3% on pre-weekend fund demand before easing slightly in late afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  Yields on two-year bonds rose one basis point to 2.28%, while five-year bond yields ended flat at 2.95%. \par \par \uc2  At the longer end of the curve, the yield on the seven-year bond was flat, at 3.33%, while the yield on the 10-year bond rose one basis point to end 3.55%. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer ended three basis point higher\par at 3.55%, and the 10-year swap offer also rose three basis points, to\par 3.77%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer narrowed to 106\par basis point from 108 basis points late Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Friday        Late Thursday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        2.7500%  2.8750%   2.1250%  2.3750%\par 3 Month          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 