\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Higher Late, But Off High After Trade Data \b0\par\par\uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2001\par 11:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher late Thursday tracking an overnight rise in the yen and on unwinding of long U.S. dollar positions by banks, dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0843 GMT (4:43 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8095, down from S$1.8129 late Thursday in Asia. It traded between S$1.8073 and S$1.8105. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower, falling below S$1.8100 in opening trade on selling by local banks and as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked the stronger yen. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had drifted lower overnight as market participants turned wary after Singapore Finance Minister Richard Hu said Wednesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outflows for mergers and acquisitions by Singapore companies weren't large enough to hurt the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly. \par \par \uc2  The publication of weaker-than-expected key trade data for Singapore briefly lifted the U.S. dollar from its intraday low. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's April non-oil domestic exports fell a nominal 1.2% from a year earlier, compared with a revised 2.2% contraction in March, the Trade Development Board said. \par \par \uc2  Economists had expected, on average, about 4.7% growth in non-oil exports for April, although individual estimates of eight economists survey by Dow Jones Newswires ranged between 1.2% to 9% growth. \par \par \uc2  But much of the U.S. dollar's partial recovery came later in the afternoon following the yen's fall to Y123.30 to a U.S. dollar after it failed to sustain a brief strengthening above Y123. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to be supported at S$1.8060 overnight, with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears forming resistance at S$1.8120 \par \par \uc2  Prices of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds ended mostly higher, though longer-dated bonds surrendered early gains on profit-taking, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Overall, trading in Singapore government bonds was light. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the two-year bond was two basis points lower at 2.28%, while the yield on the five-year bond yields was one basis point lower at 2.98%. \par \par \uc2  On the longer end of the curve, the yield on the seven-year bond was one basis point lower at 3.34% and 10-year bonds were unchanged at 3.56%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and\par are subject to minor changes. Historical bond yields and prices can be\par viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:\par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg/sgs}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore dollar interest rate swaps tracked bond yields, with the\par swap curve steeper late Thursday, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer ended unchanged at 3.53% while\par the 10-year swap offer was one basis point lower at 3.75%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer was at 114 basis\par points, wider than 113 basis points Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Interbank money market rates were steady to lower amid flush liquidity\par in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Thursday      Late Wednesday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        1.7500%  2.0000%   1.7500%  2.0000%\par 3 Month          2.0625%  2.1875%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.1875%  2.3125%   2.1875%  2.3125%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 421 4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx5h02843\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
