\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: Singapore Dlr Up As MAS, Govt Talk Tough On Policy \b0\par\par\uc2  By Amit Prakash \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2001\par 11:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar rebounded Wednesday after the government reiterated that it won't tolerate excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness arising from merger and acquisition-related outflows. \par \par \uc2  Breaking from tradition, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore confirmed what many in the market already suspected - that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a new 11-year low of S$1.8268 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The MAS also warned of further \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to offset any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness arising from corporate demand for U.S. dollars to fund overseas acquisitions and it repeated its standing policy allowing "a gradual, modest appreciation of the trade-weighted Singapore dollar." \par \par \uc2  That policy is based on its "medium-term assessment of the economy, not short-term economic or inflation developments," the MAS noted. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Finance Minister Richard Hu told parliament that recent overseas acquisitions by Singapore Telecommunications Ltd. (P.SGT) and DBS Group Holdings Ltd. (P.DBS) won't be large enough to hurt the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the amounts are sufficiently large enough to move the Sing dollar in a significant way," Hu said, adding that the MAS will continue to iron out short term volatility arising from the two deals. \par \par \uc2  Taken together, the comments prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to cover short positions in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Recently, many analysts had speculated that the MAS might have quietly altered its position on the local dollar to one tacitly approving a modest depreciation in hopes that a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would offset the negative impact on exports of slowing economic growth overseas. \par \par \uc2  The timing of the remarks caught many traders off guard, as they came just before key April trade data Thursday and revised figures for first quarter gross domestic product Friday. Both sets of data are expected to provide further evidence of Singapore's economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  While the statements are expected to ease pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near term, some analysts say that a sharp economic slowdown here might persuade the MAS to ease its monetary policy stance at its next review scheduled for July. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, the remarks appeared to signal that MAS wasn't happy with the recent pace of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. The Singapore dollar has been weakening all year, and the pace of depreciation picked up last week after the MAS said inflation isn't likely to rise in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Market participants had interpreted this comment as a hint that the MAS would abandon its moderately tight monetary policy stance, since inflation management is the sole declared objective of its monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar began the day trading around S$1.8165 and fell on the MAS acknowledgment of last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Then, after the remarks from Hu, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose further, pushing the dollar down to S$1.8128 from S$1.8146 immediately before the comments. \par \par \uc2  Around 0800 GMT the Singapore dollar was quoted at S$1.8128 to one U.S. dollar compared with S$1.8186 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The stronger local dollar caused interbank money market rates to ease slightly. Government bond prices rose marginally. \par \par \uc2  A growing number of Singapore corporations have been looking to make overseas acquisitions to diversify and expand their businesses. This puts downward pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as these companies need to buy dollars or other foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fund these deals. \par \par \uc2  In March, SingTel successfully bid for the purchase of Australia's Cable & Wireless Optus (A.CWO) in a deal valued up to A$17.3 billion. Last month, in a deal valued around S$10 billion, DBS won a bid to purchase Hong Kong's Dao Heng Bank (H.DHB). \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx5g025fi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
