\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Take a breather ahead of big day. \b0\par\par\uc2 936 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2001\par 03:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 14 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were sticking to the safety of their recent ranges early Monday ahead of the Federal Open Market Committee meeting. \par \par \uc2  Market players were likely to hold off on taking fresh positions before the FOMC meeting on Tuesday. A Reuters poll of U.S. primary dealers showed them almost unanimously expecting a 50 basis point cut from the Fed. \par \par \uc2  Positive data from the U.S. last Friday might brighten the session for regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as Asia looks to the U.S. for any pick-up in growth. U.S. April retail sales rose 0.8 percent while the University of Michigan consumer sentiment index climbed to 92.6 in May. \par \par \uc2  However the euro's travails may dampen sentiment towards the Singapore dollar while the yen offered little guidance as it barely budged against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  This week is also an eventful one in the region. Manila markets were closed for Senate and local elections, which are viewed as a key test of President Gloria Arroyo's popularity. \par \par \uc2  Singapore will issue key economic data later this week, including April non-oil domestic exports on Thursday and the first quarter economic survey on Friday. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR The Sing dollar kept a softer tone at 1.8170 against 1.8125 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said various names hesitantly bought U.S. dollars early, but the market was still on alert for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They added the MAS was most likely to come in once the Sing dollar breached 1.82, where moderate stop-loss U.S. dollar buying orders were said to be lined up. \par \par \uc2  Last week the MAS was believed to have sold at least US$500 million when the Sing dollar hit fresh 11-year lows near 1.8270. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso was quoted slightly lower offshore with domestic markets closed for congressional and local elections. Trading will resume on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Arroyo hopes to bolster her administration by winning control of the Senate after she swept to power in January when former president Joseph Estrada was ousted by a "people power" revolt. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  Hopes for foreign direct investment lifted the won's spirits in early trade as positive news on the corporate front emerged. \par \par \uc2  Optimism that General Motors Corp. would soon announce a deal to buy some of the assets of the bankrupt Daewoo Motor boosted sentiment last week. \par \par \uc2  And a local newspaper reported that debt-ridden Hynix Semiconductor may sell managerial rights and a stake to three U.S. investment funds. \par \par \uc2  But local corporates were still showing an appetite for dollars on dips below 1,300. Trading was also cautious ahead of the FOMC meeting. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  Dollar bulls continued to dominate the rupiah market as negative political developments outweighed hopes the IMF might soon resume lending to Jakarta from its $5 billion loan programme. \par \par \uc2  President Abdurrahman Wahid on Friday defied opponents, refusing to either hand over more power to his popular deputy or answer a second parliament censure over two graft scandals. \par \par \uc2  Political leaders and some cabinet ministers have been pressing for power sharing between Wahid and Vice President Megawati Sukarnoputri to end the political gridlock. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained robust and they expected dollar buying at any retracement, with 12,000 seen as a near-term target. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar was steady with foreign inflows and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support likely to keep it bound within a 32.88-32.92 range. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers in Singapore said the Nasdaq's lower finish on Friday, poor economic data last week, and a fire in an office complex in northern Taiwan's Eastern Science Park over the weekend would hurt sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The fire gutted several tech companies' offices including many subsidiaries of Acer Group which said the fire caused over $20 million in damage. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht edged down in line with other regionals but dealers in Singapore said the lack of fresh domestic leads would keep it trapped within a 45.40-45.60 band. \par \par \uc2  They said export-related inflows and unwinding of dollar longs by offshore players, following the Singapore dollar's rise last week, had supported the baht. \par \par \uc2  But sentiment was depressed by the poor economic outlook and shades of political uncertainty as a court hearing resumes on Tuesday against Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra, accused of not fully declaring his assets. \par \par \uc2  An upside break of 45.40 was likely to push the baht up to 45.20, beyond which demand for the dollar was believed to be strong, a dealer at a European bank in Singapore said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0129 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     122.53\tab  122.63\tab    +0.08\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8163\tab  1.8138\tab    -0.14\par  Taiwan dlr    32.875\tab  32.882\tab    +0.02\par  Korean won   1296.00\tab 1300.7