\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Firmer, bracing for US data, Fed. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,179 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2001\par 10:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 10 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recouped some ground in late Thursday trade on the back of a steadier yen, with some players trimming long dollar positions ahead of key U.S. data and the Federal Reserve's meeting next week. \par \par \uc2  The market was awaiting the release of weekly U.S. jobless claims data at 1230 GMT. Economists polled by Reuters estimated on average the number of new jobless claims totalled 417,000 last week against 421,000 a week earlier. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been quite long of dollars. Players seem reluctant to build fresh positions ahead of the data and the Fed's meeting. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should move within a narrow range in the runup to May 15," said a senior dealer at an Asian bank. \par \par \uc2  News Asia was moving closer to concluding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  safety net deals backed by Japan which would guard against a repeat of 1997/98 regional crisis also provided some psychological support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Japan announced it had struck a $3 billion swap deal with Thailand, a $2 billion agreement with Thailand and a $1 billion facility with Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar gained amid rumours the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again after a similar move on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was out of sync with its regional peers, losing nearly 1.50 percent on the day, as renewed political tension triggered corporate buying of dollars. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR The Sing dollar bounced back more than half a cent to 1.8175 in a tense session from lows of 1.8241 on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar sales by a U.S. bank at around S$1.8220/30 in thin lunchtime trading spurred talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market after Wednesday's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pull it back from a low of around 1.8267. \par \par \uc2  The MAS declined to comment on the talk. "We do not normally comment on our market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," an MAS official told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar has been pressured mainly by prospects of further M&A-related capital outflows, falling to fresh 11-year lows around 1.8270 earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Speculation is rife whether or not the MAS is going to shift its tightening monetary stance at a policy review in July. \par \par \uc2  Some say the MAS would shift its policy to neutral, guiding the Sing dollar lower to buoy the export-oriented economy. Others argue the Sing dollar on a trade-weighted basis was already slipping into a neutral policy band, removing the need for them to further ease their stance. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah eased to one-week lows amid hightened political tensions and after a meeting of key politicians due on Friday was postponed. \par \par \uc2  However the downside was capped at around 11,250-11,270 where Jakarta dealers said state banks were seen selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the shaky political situation after the second censure of President Abdurrahman Wahid by parliament and poor economic outlook would continue to weigh down the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  They said the delay of the meeting, expected to come up with a power sharing formula to ease tension, disappointed the market. \par \par \uc2  Local newspapers also quoted army chief General Endriartono Sutarto warning Wahid not to sack parliament. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht barely moved in listless sideways trading, stuck in a tight range over the course of the day. \par \par \uc2  Bangkok dealers said petrochemical importers were looking to sell the baht at around 45.50, but buying by exporters was underpinning it near 45.60. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was not worried about inflation in 2001 and would keep its forecast range of 0-3.5 percent for inflation this year despite fluctuating oil prices. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso ended slightly higher from Wednesday's 50.65 close helped by some export-related inflows and a lack of overall dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the peso gapped down at the open on expected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buying, which they believed had pulled the peso down late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  It touched a high of 50.47 where importers and players wary over Monday's Senate polls were seen bidding for dollars. \par \par \uc2  The polls widely viewed as a referendum on President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo's polularity, were also seen as key to the Philippines' credit rating outlook. \par \par \uc2  The head of Standard and Poor's sovereign ratings John Chambers said if the president did not get a strong mandate, governability would be an issue for her for the rest of her term. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won bowed slightly due to a softer yen, but stayed in a tight 1,304.5-1,308 range as the yen's movements were minimal. \par \par \uc2  Since the beginning of the week, the won has been trapped in increasingly narrow price action. Dealers said while local importers have been seeking dollars on dips, exporter dollar sales have capped the won's losses. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors' stock purchases have also provided underlying support for the won. Foreign investors bought a net 80.97 billion won worth of shares on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But the yen was still the dominant influence, and analysts say without major movements in dollar/yen and few fresh local factors, the won was likely to stick to narrow ranges. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar drooped with the softer yen, but stayed well within its recent range of 32.86-32.90. \par \par \uc2  With the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on both sides, dealers said interest in the market was dwindling, and the Taiwan dollar would likely be trapped in its recent range for some time. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Taiwan's slowing economy also signalled it was unlikely for the curreny to strengthen and some expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to let it weaken. \par \par \uc2  "The continued slowing both in monetary growth as well as bank lending also point to tighter domestic liquidity ahead," said Standard Chartered Bank in a research note. \par \par \uc2  "The sluggishness in the economy's near term outlook will hence keep the TWD rangebound for now with TWD gains capped at 32.80 in coming weeks." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0848 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     122.25\tab  121.57\tab    -0.56\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8185\tab  1.8210\tab    +0.14\par  Taiwan dlr    32.884\tab  32.879\tab    -0.02\par  Korean won   1300.00\tab 1301.70\tab    +0.13\par  Baht\tab      45.47\tab   45.49\tab    +0.04\par  Peso\tab      50.45\tab   50.65\tab    +0.40\par  Rupiah\tab 11170.00     11070.00\tab    -0