\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Dn;Singapore Dlr At Mercy Of M&As \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2001\par 11:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  drifted slightly lower late Wednesday, but remained hemmed in within narrow ranges, as they mirrored the yen's movements, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Resisting the yen's pressure, however, the Singapore dollar and the Thai baht were stronger. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar took centerstage on renewed concerns that Singapore Telecommunication Ltd.'s A$17 billion bid for Australia's Cable & Wireless Optus (A.CWO) might be scuttled. \par \par \uc2  A report by the Melbourne Age newspaper that the Australian government will block SingTel's planned takeover of C&W Optus, unless the U.S. grants export licenses for military satellite technology, resurrected such fears. \par \par \uc2  The deal, which has contributed to the U.S. dollar's recent strength against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , led to a sharp pullback in the U.S. dollar to around S$1.8200 in early Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  Given the magnitude of the deal - it would be the largest overseas acquisition by a Singapore company - the local dollar has been sensitive to any news of its progress. \par \par \uc2  "The market keeps vacillating about the SingTel-Optus deal," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  As the concerns ebbed, the U.S. dollar quickly regained its footing. Interbank participants scooped for bargains, even before SingTel stepped in to say it was confident the deal will go through, following talks with Australian and U.S. government officials on its acquisition of C&W Optus. \par \par \uc2  "The pullback triggered an opportunity to buy cheap dollars," said a dealer at a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  A large local bank, which has been snapping up U.S. dollars in recent sessions, unloaded the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late trade, pushing it down to S$1.8216 at 0910 GMT Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday, the U.S. dollar was higher at S$1.8238. \par \par \uc2  Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which dealers believed had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth the local dollar's decline last Friday and earlier this week - also kept participants wary of pushing the Singapore dollar down too aggressively. \par \par \uc2  "There's a hint of repetition about today's (Wednesday's) Singapore dollar story," Bank of America said in a research note. "The world and his dog wants to buy dollar-Sing, but there is a whiff of the authorities at S$1.8250 and above." \par \par \uc2  A marginal rise in Malaysia's international reserves as of April 30, and reassurance by a senior government official that the government has no plans to review the ringgit peg for now assuaged fears of an imminent devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This also contributed to the Singapore dollar's sharp rebound in early trading, market watchers said. \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx590216w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
