\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Dn Late,But Off Low;  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Suspected \b0\par\par\uc2 560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2001\par 10:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was slightly lower late Tuesday on continued corporate demand for U.S. dollars, but came off its early low amid suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0819 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8244, up from S$1.8234 late Friday in Asia, but was lower than its intraday high at S$1.8265. \par \par \uc2  Singapore markets were closed Monday for a public holiday, but in offshore dealings, U.S. dollar hit a new 11-year high of S$1.8268 against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in thin, illiquid trading. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened around S$1.8160 in Asia Tuesday and rose quickly to S$1.8265 in early trades before retreating on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through a large local bank, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  For the remainder of the day, the U.S. dollar traded between S$1.8236 and S$1.8250, with its upside capped by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears and a slightly stronger Thai baht. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said demand for U.S. dollars from companies and for mergers and acquisitions is still strong, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to be bought on dips. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is well supported at S$1.8200 overnight, with resistance at S$1.8300, dealers said \par \par \uc2  In a morning research note, Singapore's DBS bank said it expects the U.S. dollar to trade between S$1.8200 and S$1.8400 this week. \par \par \uc2  On the bond market, prices of government bonds ended slightly lower on position squaring by banks following last week's lackluster trading. \par \par \uc2  "The prices have been going nowhere and there hasn't been much fresh news to trade on, so some players decided to square their longs," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the two-year bond was three basis points higher at 2.32%, while the five-year bond ended one basis point higher at 3.01%. \par \par \uc2  On the longer end of the curve, the yield on the seven- and 10-year papers rose by two basis points each, to 3.40% and 3.59%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historic bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore Web Site: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  https://secure.mas.gov.sg/dataroom/dataroom_findbindex.html\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the Singapore dollar interest rate swaps market, swaps rose\par slightly as higher bond yields and a weaker Singapore dollar triggered\par paying interest across the curve.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap offer rose three basis points to\par 3.54%, while the 10-year swap rose two basis points to 3.75%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer narrowed to 104\par basis points from 105 basis points Friday, resulting in a slightly\par flatter swap curve.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the interbank money market, a weaker Singapore dollar pushed the\par overnight and short term rates higher, but the longer dated rates\par remained steady.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Tuesday       Late Friday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        3.3750%  2.5000%   1.0000%  1.1250%\par 3 Month          2.1875%  2.3125%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx5802355\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
