\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Steady yen helps, U.S. data awaited. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,112 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2001\par 04:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 4 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were steadier early Friday as a buoyant yen sparked hopes that countries in the region might not need to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak to stay competitive in export markets. \par \par \uc2  But yen-inspired gains were expected to be counteracted by U.S. jobless claims released overnight that showed record corporate layoffs, raising fears the worst might not be over. \par \par \uc2  The market is now focused on the April U.S. employment report due at 1230 GMT, which will give a clearer picture of the labour market. \par \par \uc2  Employment is closely linked to consumer confidence - the linchpin in the U.S. economy, which is the biggest market for exports from Asia. \par \par \uc2  Economic data in the region was also not encouraging. Singapore's Purchasing Managers' Index (PMI) for April showed a reading of 46.2 percent, compared with 47.4 percent in March. But position squaring ahead of a long weekend holiday in several centres should help underpin the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the market is generally long on dollar. \par \par \uc2  Markets in India, Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore and Thailand will be closed on Monday to celebrate Buddha's birthday. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won broke 1,293 resistance, though importers' dollar bids brought it off the high so far of 1,287. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a clean break of 1,293, last seen on March 21, paves the way towards 1,284, which is the 62 percent Fibonacci retracement of the dollar's rise from February 21 to April 4. \par \par \uc2  However, the market has so far hesitated from really testing key resistance given the yen's modest gains and thin trade. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have also said the won's rise might be short-lived given Korea's slowing economy. Finance Minister Jin Nyum said earlier the economy was expected to grow by 5 percent in 2001, adding "artificial" measures were needed to achieve that. \par \par \uc2  The OECD said on Thursday it forecast 4.2 percent growth for the Korean economy in 2001, with a U.S.-led rebound possible if vital corporate reforms continue to be pursued. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar bobbed around 1.8200 after hitting a 1.8218 low overnight on what was believed to be M&A-related flows. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cap the Sing's downside but more signs of a weaker economy were likely to dampen sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's Purchasing Managers' Index for April stood at 46.2 percent, from 47.4 percent in March. However, the electronics sector gained a marginal 0.6 point, which indicated a possible bottoming out. \par \par \uc2  The PMI followed government surveys of outlooks in the manufacturing, commerce and services for April-September that showed deteriorating sentiment towards business conditions. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said some local and foreign banks were still looking to buy dollars, possibly bringing about a test 1.8220, which the market belives would be defended by the MAS. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was struggling to stay above 11,000, with persistent local corporate dollar demand amid Indonesia's slippery political scene. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said the market continued to prefer holding dollars given the political uncertainty shrouding the country. Parliament recently handed President Abdurrahman Wahid a second censure, and if his reply is not viewed as satisfactory, impeachment proceedings could be started. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Jakarta's thorny politics was holding up key reforms, including budget revisions needed in order to receive a long-delayed $400 million loan from the IMF. \par \par \uc2  The government said late Thursday it had agreed to recommendations by an independent panel on changing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, which had been a sticking point with the Fund. It did not give further detail. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht was treading water, with demand for it tapering off after one big bank in Singapore was spotted buying a large amount of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Equity-linked inflows also supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with foreign investors turning net buyers of local stocks since April 27, according to data from the Stock Exchange of Thailand. \par \par \uc2  But the market was unlikely to push the baht aggressively in either direction ahead of a long weekend holiday. Thai financial markets will be closed on Monday and Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The one-month forward rate was steady at 6/10 satang per dollar in the offshore market and -9.5/-9.0 satang in the onshore market. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso was likely to feel some pressure as Moody's Investors Service said it was concerned about the Philippines negative rating outlook after the state of rebellion imposed by the government to crush opposition protests. \par \par \uc2  Moody's said on Thursday it had maintained the Philippine's Ba1 foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating as it believes the country is amply cushioned against external economic shocks over the near term. \par \par \uc2  But it added that unresolved political uncertainties will adversely affect the country's longer-term economic prospects. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Singapore said with the market slightly short of dollars after the peso's gains in the past few sessions, a volatile session could be expected. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar opened higher at 32.868 against the Thursday's close of 32.890 on the back of follow-through buying linked to investment in local stocks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the upside could be limited by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been in the market lately to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains. Some believe the authority is likely to keep it at 32.82-32.85 in the near term. \par \par \uc2  The market awaited April consumer price data due on Monday. March CPI rose 0.44 percent on-year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also scheduled to release end-April \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves figures on Monday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0212 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     121.12\tab  121.59\tab    +0.39\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8205\tab  1.8193\tab    -0.07\par  Taiwan dlr     32.88\tab   32.89\tab    +0.04\par  Korean won   1294.00\tab 1294.40\tab    +0.03\par  Baht\tab      45.44\tab   45.52\tab    +0.18\par  Peso\tab      50.18\tab   49.74\tab    -0.88\par  Rupiah\tab 10970.00     11000.00\tab    +0.27\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     121.12\tab  114.45\tab    -5.51\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8205\tab  1.7365\tab    -4.61\par  Taiwan dlr     32.82\tab   32.90\tab    +0.24\par  Korean won   1294.00\tab 1277.00\tab    -1.31\par  