\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late; Revisits 11-Yr Low \b0\par\par\uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2001\par 10:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar fell further late Friday, revisiting its recent 11-year low of S$1.8245 against the U.S dollar on strong U.S. dollar buying led by local banks on behalf of companies. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement that Singapore's inflation rate was unlikely to rise in the near term, despite the weak Singapore dollar, helped precipitate the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar retreated from its 11-year high amid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears and was quoted at S$1.8234 at 0838 GMT (4:38 a.m. EDT), up from S$1.8201 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Reversing their earlier buying spree, local banks subsequently sold U.S. dollars in late Asian trading, suggesting that the selloff was likely at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behest, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The last suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurred last week when the U.S. dollar first hit its 11-year high. \par \par \uc2  At 0925 GMT (5:25 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.8202, little changed from late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower in Asia on selling by European banks in the wake of a stronger yen. But its early decline was short-lived, as a large local bank, which has been an aggressive buyer this week, resumed buying U.S. dollars on behalf of companies and for merger and acquisition-related outflows. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's early rise was punctuated by sales from U.S. and European banks executing sell orders on behalf of clients. \par \par \uc2  A second wave of U.S. dollar buying emerged following a statement by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore that inflation was unlikely to rise further near-term and that domestic price stability isn't threatened by a weaker local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Market participants interpreted the statement as a hint that the MAS may ease its moderately tight monetary policy stance at its next scheduled review in July, a senior dealer at a European bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Inflation, as measured by the Consumer Price Index, rose 1.8% from a year ago in March and was up 0.2% from February. The March inflation rate was at the high end of the MAS' 1% to 2% tolerance band. \par \par \uc2  With local banks leading the buying, market participants were emboldened to bid the U.S. dollar higher to its recent 11-year high, inviting the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The MAS usually doesn't comment on its market actions. \par \par \uc2  Long Bonds End Higher \par \par \uc2  Prices of longer-dated government bonds ended higher, tracking overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys on expected poor U.S. unemployment data later Friday, and on a further contraction in Singapore's Purchasing Managers' Index for April, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The April PMI fell 1.2 percentage points to 46.2%, indicating that manufacturing activity in Singapore contracted for the third consecutive month. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the two-year bond was flat at 2.29%, while the five-year bond ended one basis point lower at 2.99%. \par \par \uc2  On the longer end of the curve, the yield on the seven- and 10-year papers fell by two basis points each, to 3.38% and 3.57%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historic bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore Web Site: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  https://secure.mas.gov.sg/dataroom/dataroom_findbindex.html\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the Singapore dollar interest rate swaps market, the swap curve\par flattened, with the longer end declining on receiving interest and the\par short-end being paid higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap fell two basis points to 3.51%, while\par the 10-year swap fell two basis points to 3.73%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer narrowed to 105\par basis points from 109 basis points Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the interbank money market, the overnight offered rate rose to an\par intraday high of 3.25% on pre-weekend demand for funds, but retreated as\par liquidity returned toward the close of trading. Term rates remained\par steady, however.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Friday        Late Thursday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        1.0000%  1.2500%   0.8750%  1.1250%\par 3 Month          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 04/05/01 10-18G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx5401zvj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
