\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Firm as dlr consolidates, US data eyed. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2001\par 11:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, April 3 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were firmer in late trade on Thursday as market participants trimmed dollar longs in the face of a firm yen and pending U.S. job data. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Golden Week holidays thinned trading volumes in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Markets in much of Southeast and South Asia will be closed for a public holiday on Monday and dealers said players were squaring positions, fearing they might be caught on the wrong side when trading resumes on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expected regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stay in tight ranges ahead of the U.S. government's weekly jobless claims data at 1230 GMT and the April unemployment report on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. is a key market for Asia's exports and any news that points to its ability to absorb goods from the region is supportive to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Although consumer spending has been quite resilient so far this year, recent jobless claims have risen rather sharply," said Syetarn Hansakul, economist at WestLB in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "This raised fears of an employment-led downturn. Any sharp rise in unemployment in April would dampen consumer confidence and that could adversely affect Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," she said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar bucked the trend on Thursday, slipping under the weight of dollar demand linked to M&A activities as local companies seek to expand by buying into overseas firms. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso made good gains after street protests to support jailed former president Joseph Estrada were brought rapidly under control earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR The Sing dollar slipped to session lows of 1.8213 having spent most of the day orbiting a tight 1.8160-1.8194 range. Dealers said local banks and U.S. names sold the Sing dollar against the U.S. dollar and the Australian dollar, fanning talk they were still executing orders for a deal by Singapore Telecommunications to buy Australia's Optus . \par \par \uc2  The Aussie/Sing dollar cross , which has been in a steep uptrend channel since April 24, reached three-month highs of 0.9498 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expected firm support for the Sing dollar around 1.8200 to the U.S. dollar on fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the Sing dollar might face fresh downward pressure if Singapore's Purchasing Managers' Index for April, due on Friday, reinforced concerns about a slowing economy. \par \par \uc2  In March the PMI index shrank to 47.4, contracting for the second straight month for the first time since January 1999. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso extended its gains, reaching a high of 50.10, up nearly three percent from 15-week lows of 51.60 early Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Manila dealers said power companies and oil firms were looking to buy the U.S. dollar around 50.20, but the broader market was keen to book profit on long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the market was keeping a wary eye on the political situation, especially Senate elections on May 14, when President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo needs to capture enough seats to ensure a solid majority. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept key overnight rates unchanged, a move widely expected by the markets. The overnight borrowing rate is currently at 9.50 percent and lending at 11.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Philippine merchandise exports fell for the second consecutive month in March, pointing to slower growth in the economy in the first quarter compared to the end of last year. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won climbed past 1,300 thanks to the firmer yen, but importers' persistent dollar bids stopped it from testing the key resistance level of 1,293. \par \par \uc2  With the yen only making modest gains, dealers said the market was reluctant to test 1,293, last touched on March 21 when the dollar broke through 1,300. \par \par \uc2  They added if the yen rallied above 121.30, then the market might find the resolve to test 1,293. \par \par \uc2  Settlement of stock purchases by foreign funds gave the won a boost. Foreign investors have bought an impressive net 463.6 billion won worth of local stock in the past three sessions. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said for now, the won's rise remained precarious given slowing external demand for Korea's exports. Recent data showed exports fell by 9.3 percent on year in April. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht inched up further on continued trimming of dollar longs by players caught short in the offshore market, Singapore dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Singapore and Hong Kong names led the dollar sales but local corporate dollar demand limited the baht's upside. \par \par \uc2  "The baht is tracking the yen much closer as there is a lack of fresh local leads and that is making a lot of foreign players a bit uncomfortable with their dollar longs," a dealer at a European bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  He said players were also squaring off before a long weekend. Thai financial markets will be closed on Monday and Tuesday for the birthday of Buddha and for Coronation Day. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah fluctuated within the 11,000-11,300 band with some export-related inflows causing trimming of dollar longs early and local corporate dollar demand capping the upside. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said speculation embattled president Abdurrahman Wahid was losing support also kept players cautious. \par \par \uc2  They said the market was keenly watching the political situation after parliament handed Wahid a second censure for his alleged involvement in graft scandals. \par \par \uc2  Local media reported Wahid's ministers told him to hold his tongue and share power with other political parties through a cabinet revamp if he wanted to survive in office. \par \par \uc2  Wahid has a month to reply to the parliamentary rebuke and if his reply is not found satisfactory, impeachment proceedings could be initiated. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar trod familiar ground, spending the session in the week's range so far of 32.858-928. \par \par \uc2  Hopes for foreign equity inflows and a firmer yen helped it rack up some gains earlier in the day, but it soon surrendered these amid wariness over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been recently spotted in the market buying dollars to cap its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, in what analysts see as an effort to keep exports competitive given slowing demand from the U.S. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0858 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     121.66\tab  121.91\tab    +0.21\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8198\tab  1.8180\tab    -0.10\par  Taiwan dlr    32.890\tab  32.900\tab    +0.03\par  Korean won   1294.40\tab 1301.00\tab    +0.51\par  Baht\tab      45.42\tab   45.45\tab    +0.07\par  Peso\tab      49.74\tab   50.73\tab    +1.99\par  Rupiah\tab 