\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late; Local Bks Buy US Dlrs For Cos, M&A \b0\par\par\uc2 532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2001\par 10:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower late Thursday, as local banks continued to buy U.S. dollars, most likely on behalf of companies and to make payments for overseas asset acquisitions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0818 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8201, up from S$1.8198 late Wednesday in Asia. It traded between S$1.8160 and S$1.8105. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower, around S$1.8160, in Asia following overnight losses amid suspicions among some market participants that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in London. \par \par \uc2  However the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose from the opening bell as local banks resumed buying U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  "The demand for U.S. dollars was quite strong and was most likely on behalf of companies and, possibly, also for (merger and acquisition-related) outflows," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  According a European bank dealer, some interbank participants also took the opportunity to cover short U.S. dollar positions and to cut losses. \par \par \uc2  "With Japan closed, the dollar/yen traders (were) watching the dollar/Sing and (were) jumping on any opportunity to test MAS sensitivity on the upside," a U.S. brokerage dealer said. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. dollar's upside was capped by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, given that the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained stable through the day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the U.S. dollar to face initial resistance at S$1.8210 and then at S$1.8245, with support at S$1.8150. \par \par \uc2  Singapore government bond prices ended slightly higher, with local banks continuing to support prices, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local banks are currently placing sizable corporate issues with institutional investors, they explained. \par \par \uc2  However, trading remained thin amid a lack of significant market-affecting news. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the two-year bond fell one basis point to 2.29%, while the five-year ended one basis point lower at 3.0%. \par \par \uc2  On the longer end of the curve, the yield on the seven- and 10-year papers fell by one basis point each, to 3.40% and 3.59%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historic bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore Web Site: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  https://secure.mas.gov.sg/dataroom/dataroom_findbindex.html\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the Singapore dollar interest rate swaps market, swap offers\par drifted lower as interbank money market rates remained soft, amid ample\par liquidity in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap fell one basis point to 3.53%, while\par the 10-year swap fell two basis points, to 3.74%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer narrowed to 109\par basis points from 110 basis points Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Thursday      Late Wednesday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        0.8750%  1.0000%   0.8750%  1.1250%\par 3 Month          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \