\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late As Bks Buy US Dlrs; Gives Up Gains \b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2001\par 10:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower late Wednesday compared with late Monday, after surrendering its overnight gains in a choppy session amid U.S. dollar buying led by a large local bank, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0819 GMT (4:19 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8198, up from S$1.8182 late Monday in Asia. It traded between S$1.8115 and S$1.8215. \par \par \uc2  Singapore markets were closed Tuesday for May Day. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia, reflecting its overnight losses in London and New York in the wake of a stronger yen. \par \par \uc2  It fell to an intraday low of S$1.8115 in opening trade, but, in a quick reversal of fortunes, rose sharply on large-scale U.S. dollar buying led by a large local bank. \par \par \uc2  This large local bank "would easily have bought around US$200 million today," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said the U.S. dollar buying was on behalf of the bank's corporate clients, while others sought to link the buying to unconfirmed market talk that the local bank had renewed its interest in the credit card business of a South Korean bank. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rose to S$1.8195 by midmorning as interbank market participants joined the buying spree following its rise above Y122. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar sales by a U.S. bank and European investment funds amid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears stalled the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Its subsequent retreat below Y122 pushed it lower against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well, but the U.S. dollar rallied again to briefly hit an intraday high of S$1.8215 in late afternoon trading before retreating on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the U.S. dollar to be well supported at S$1.8150, with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears capping its upside at S$1.8220. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bond prices ended higher, with some local banks believed to have supported prices, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Local banks are currently placing sizable corporate issues with institutional investors, they explained. \par \par \uc2  However, trading remained thin amid a lack of significant market-affecting news. \par \par \uc2  The yields on the two- and five-year papers both fell two basis points to 2.30% and 3.04%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  On the longer end of the curve, the yield on the seven- and one-year papers fell by two basis points as well, to 3.41% and 3.60%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and are subject to minor changes. Historic bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore Web Site: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  https://secure.mas.gov.sg/dataroom/dataroom_findbindex.html\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the Singapore dollar interest rate swaps market, swap offers\par drifted lower as interbank money market rates remained soft, amid ample\par liquidity in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap fell three basis points to 3.54%. The\par 10-year swap was also down three basis points, to 3.76%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer widened to 110\par basis points from 109 basis points Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Wednesday     Late Monday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        0.8750%  1.1250%   0.7500%  1.0000%\par 3 Month          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.3125%  2.4375%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx5201xpv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
