\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Tad Lower Late, Local Bank Buying US Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2001\par 10:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was slightly lower in the pre-holiday market late Monday, on U.S. dollar buying by local banks on behalf of companies, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0852 GMT (4:52 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8182, up from S$1.8180 late Friday in Asia. It traded in a narrow range between S$1.8180 and S$1.8202. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar briefly rose above S$1.8200 on buying by a large local bank, most likely on behalf of its corporate clients, but retreated once the buying ebbed, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late afternoon position-squaring by interbank participants ahead of the holiday also caused the dollar to retreat. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was thin due to holidays in Japan, Hong Kong and ahead of the May Day holiday in Singapore Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to be well supported at S$1.8170 by demand from companies, particularly those in the process of overseas acquisitions. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's upside is expected to be capped by\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fears at it's 11-year high of S$1.8245. This was the level\par at which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend the\par local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week.\par - - 30/04/01 08-56G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore government bond prices were steady to slightly higher in\par extremely thin month-end trading. Only a handful of trades were dealt in\par the three-month, seven- and ten-year tenors, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Interest rate swaps, particularly at the short-end of the curve,\par drifted lower on increased selling interest amid softer interbank money\par market rates, as liquidity returned to the banking system after the\par weekend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Last week someone was squeezing the (money) market at the short-end,\par and rates and IRS went up. With that squeeze gone, rated and IRS are\par coming off," a dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Short-dated swaps were also pushed lower by recent cuts in\par home-mortgage rates by local banks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on the two-year paper tracked short-dated swaps and fell two\par basis points to 2.31%, while the yield on the five-year paper was steady\par at 3.04%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the longer end of the curve, the yields on the seven- and one-year\par papers were flat at 3.43% and 3.62%, respectively.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and\par are subject to minor changes. Historic bond yields and prices can be\par viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore Web Site.\par  https://secure.mas.gov.sg/dataroom/dataroom_findbindex.html\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the Singapore dollar interest rate swaps market, one- to three-year\par swaps eased by three to seven basis points, while longer dated swaps\par were mostly steady.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap was up one basis point at 3.57%, and\par the 10-year swap was flat from Friday's close at 3.79%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer widened to 109\par basis points from 105 basis points Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Monday        Late Friday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        0.7500%  1.0000%   3.2500%  3.5000%\par 3 Month          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.3125%  2.4375%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 30/04/01 09-40G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx4u01xy4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
