\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late In Choppy Trading Ahead Of G7 Mtg \b0\par\par\uc2 488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2001\par 10:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was slightly stronger against the U.S. dollar late Friday in choppy trade, a day after it touched a new 11-year low against the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  Trading was volatile as some players sold their long U.S. dollar positions ahead of a meeting of the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations' finance minister and central bankers. \par \par \uc2  Around 0800 GMT (4.00 a.m. Friday EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8180, down from S$1.8243 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The pair opened at around S$1.8190 on buying by a large local bank, but fell to the intraday low of S$1.8185 as some foreign investors cleared long positions ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  But the pair rose above S$1.8200 in late trading as the yen began to lose ground against the U.S. dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar also capped the U.S. dollar's gains. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Thursday after the pair peaked at the new 11-year high. Earlier this month, the MAS had reiterated that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bond prices rose Friday as investors parked their funds in the safer instrument, ahead of the G7 meeting of finance ministers and central bankers this weekend and amid rising political uncertainty in Indonesia. Yields move in the opposite direction to prices. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the two-year paper fell two basis points to 2.30%, and the yield on the five-year paper also fell two basis points, to 3.03%. \par \par \uc2  On the longer end of the curve, the yield on the seven-year paper fell three basis points to 3.42%, and the yield on the 10-year paper shed two basis points to 3.61%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The above are pre-settlement closing bids obtained from dealers and\par are subject to minor changes. Historic bond yields and prices can be\par viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore Web Site.\par  https://secure.mas.gov.sg/dataroom/dataroom_findbindex.html\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the Singapore dollar interest rate swaps market, yields fell across\par the curve.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most liquid seven-year swap was down two basis points at 3.56%,\par and the 10-year swap fell to 3.79% from 3.82% Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offer narrowed sharply to\par 105 basis points from 112 basis points late Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Friday        Late Thursday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        3.2500%  3.5000%   2.0000%  2.2500%\par 3 Month          2.2500%  2.3750%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par 1 Year           2.3125%  2.4375%   2.3125%  2.4375%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Hasan Jafri, Dow Jones Newswires; 65/421-4828;\par hasan.jafri@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx4r01w51\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
