\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2 's MAS: Ready To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  To Keep SGD In Band \b0\par\par\uc2 325 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2001\par 03:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said Tuesday it has not changed its policy of allowing a gradual, modest appreciation of the trade-weighted Singapore dollar within a policy band, as stated in its monetary policy Statement of Feb. 2001. \par \par \uc2  "Although the trade-weighted Singapore dollar has weakened since then, it has remained within the policy band. MAS stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary to keep it within the band," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  "The recent weakness of the Singapore dollar, especially against the U.S. dollar, has prompted speculation that MAS has shifted its policy stance on the exchange rate. This is not so," it added. \par \par \uc2  Following is the full text of MAS' statement: \par \par \uc2  The downward revision in GDP growth forecast for 2001 from 5%-7% to 3.5%-5.5% reflects the deteriorating outlook for the external environment. The downturns in the U.S. economy and in the global electronics industry have been deeper than expected. Our less optimistic assessment of Singapore's prospects therefore reflects a cyclical fall in external demand, rather than deterioration in our export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  The recent weakness of the Singapore dollar, especially against the U.S. dollar, has prompted speculation that MAS has shifted its policy stance on the exchange rate. This is not so. \par \par \uc2  MAS manages the Singapore dollar exchange rate against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and not against any single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . MAS has not changed its policy of allowing a gradual, modest appreciation of the trade-weighted Singapore dollar within a policy band, as stated in the MAS Monetary Policy Statement of Feb. 2001. Although the trade-weighted Singapore dollar has weakened since then, it has remained within the policy band. MAS stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary to keep it within the band. \par \par \uc2  MAS will review its policy stance in July, and will be making a statement on monetary policy then. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx4a01ntb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
