\par\par \b \uc2  ASEAN: MAS To Defend Singapore Dlr If Needed - Minister \b0\par\par\uc2 204 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2001\par 08:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore will continue to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it becomes too volatile, Singapore's second finance minister, Lim Hng Kiang suggested Sunday. \par \par \uc2  "The MAS has put a statement on the Singapore dollar - that statement still remains effective," Lim said, when asked by Dow Jones Newswires if the government is concerned by the Singapore dollar's fall to an 11-year low earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, the Singapore dollar fell to an 11-year low of S$1.8230 against its U.S. counterpart as the Japanese yen slumped and as traders speculated that the MAS would ease the moderately tightening bias of its monetary policy. Late Friday in Asia, the Singapore dollar was hovering around S$1.8184. \par \par \uc2  The de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently reiterated that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if necessary, to dampen excessive fluctuations of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, including movements arising from the funding of corporate merger and acquisition activities. \par \par \uc2  Lim was speaking to reporters at the fringes of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations finance ministers' weekend meeting. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx4801kwe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
